<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-attractor`>>

!! The Pono Roadmap ~ the attractor reads STABLE FOUNDING

''STABLE FOUNDING'' names the state the house grows toward (operator-ruled). Once a Lararium stands, its
base state reads STABLE: a PRIVATE household SEED (its own charter, one seat) for the operator's own devices, and a FOUNDED PERSONA
ZERO. Everything else flows downstream of it — contracting into other Nexuses and founding a Nexus with a second operator, inviting the operator's own
PersonaGroup devices, and hosting guests who walk.

''The first rung reads the memory-stage hearth'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31, #/rulings). Before any rung above it, one operator's node and browser stand joined on one machine, each from its anon floor through the full rites: the operator's hand performs the founding, the unlock, the browser passphrase and the join acts, and a harness walks the same rites through the test door (#/cold-start). A page edit reaches `<LAR_ROOT>/wikis/daemon/`, and the CLI and the MCP seat read and write that same wiki. The house's own design tracking and its self-improvement loops then move off untracked scratch files onto that wiki, a memory stage (#/stride-0-hearth). The rung stands up the working Lararium the live-test rulings ask for first (#/rulings, a working Lararium comes first), and every rung above it inherits the walk it proves.

This carrier holds the house's ONE roadmap, and ONLY what binds or stays owed. A cold session that
reads the boot seed and then this carrier can pick up any thread: the instruments and the law that bites
(#/cold-start), the order a free slot takes (#/the-order), the rulings (#/rulings), the design decisions that shape unbuilt work (#/decisions),
the six paths in one order (#/the-road), every open row in the stride that holds it (#/the-loops indexes the strides),
each train's commits in order (#/the-trains), the lanes that run beside the road (#/side-lanes), the fresh
start (#/fresh-start), and what the operator owns (#/open). Landed work reads in git and the
CHANGELOG, never here. No count rests here: each reading names the instrument that takes it. Law lives in
its own carrier, and this carrier points there.

''Full models land early'' (operator ruling; quote license: //"Full caps now … Full models in early, so we don't code churn later."//). The road takes no staging by milestone: a slice builds its whole model, with its whole capability reach, in the place the order gives it, and no slice ships a narrower interim that a later slice must re-cut.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-order`>>

!! The order ~ what a free slot takes next (reprioritized 2026-11-01)

Four lanes fly at most, each in its own worktree under the lane law (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/controller-handoff#/the-lane-law`>>). A free slot takes the highest rank whose files no flying lane holds; the owner law of #/the-road still names one hand per compose hunk, and GROUND, the side lanes and HARBOR's tail ride beside on disjoint files. The order ranks by what inherits shape (#/cold-start, grounds before wins): the hearth's walk gives every later lane a live node and browser to prove against, and the custody and security cures close holes before more code stands on them.

|! Rank |! Train or stride |! Rows |! State and gate |
| 1 | ''L'', the memory-stage hearth (#/stride-0-hearth) | `L1` to `L20`, with `F11` pulled forward | `L1`, `L2` and `L5` landed (PR #143; `L1` and `L2` keep their tails), `L3` (PR #148; its tail, the operator's `lares home tend`); next `L6`; `L4`, `L17` and `L8` to `L13` wait on the merge of ''R''; the operator's hand at the rites |
| 1b | ''R'', block and inline sigils at TiddlyWiki's parsing parity (#/the-trains, R; #/rulings, 2026-11-01) | one lane, merged once | opens now, since ''Q'' landed (PR #149, #/o-takiage); merges before `L4`, `L17` and `L8` to `L13`, so the memory stage takes its first tiddlers in the settled grammar and frame; its genesis bake and the deps lane's run one after the other, never beside; the canon-law lane (`T38`) follows it, so `meme check` takes one re-cut over both trains |
| 1c | The deps before the founding (#/side-lanes) | one lane, its two trials first | opens after ''Q'' (the lockfile moves); Trial A lifts automerge to 3.5.0, Trial B holds keyhive at 0.3; its bake follows ''R''s; lands before the operator founds the hearth |
| 1d | The legacy-check pyre (#/side-lanes) | two read waves, then one lane | gated: opens once the house stands and the MCP seat answers (the operator's founding and `lares home tend`), after ''R'' and the deps lane land |
| 2 | ''T'' wave 0, the cures (#/stride-t-recut) | `T21` to `T29`, `T37` | `T19`, `T22` and `T27` landed (PR #141), `T20` (PR #138), `T33` to `T35` (PR #144); next `T21`, then `T23` to `T25`, then `T26`; `T37` takes a custody slot; `T28` rides `W30` |
| 3 | The keystone of ONE FACE and the seal (#/the-trains, O1) | `U2` to `U6` over `F1`, `F6`, `F10`, sealed-store step 3, `K21` | `U1` landed (PR #140); `U2` next, `U3` and `U4` beside it on disjoint files |
| 4 | ''T'' waves A and B (#/stride-t-recut) | `T2` to `T8`, `T30` to `T32`, `T36` | `T3` and `T2`'s pilot landed (PR #142); the rest as slots free behind wave 0, `T2`'s codemod and `T36` open now that ''Q'' landed; every cut row waits on its cold read and its collision read |
| 5 | Stride 1, the spine (#/stride-1-spine) | `K18`'s door, `K20`, `B1`, `W4`, `K16`, `I3`, the rest | behind ranks 1 to 4 |
| 6 | Stride 2, the floor (#/stride-2-floor) | `W26` slice 5, `P10`, `W29`'s browser ceremony, the rest | behind rank 5 where it shares ground |
| 7 | ''T'' wave C (#/stride-t-recut) | `T9` to `T18` | gated on `T6` (`u1` landed, PR #140); `T12` takes a quiet window as the one named single-owner step |
| 8 | Stride 3, the bake (#/stride-3-bake) | `G37`, `X0` | waits on each member's readiness red by name |
| 9 | After the seat, closed before the founding (#/after-the-seat) | every row there | each a lane before the founding unless a named gate holds it |
| 10 | TWO HEARTHS (#/the-trains, X) | `X1` to `X5`, the ceremony | operator gates `A2`, `A4`, `B3`, `X3` (#/operator-gates) |

''Beside the ranks'': the cells and shards rebalance's tail (#/side-lanes; `testlever` landed as PR #139, `ci-ten` as PR #145); the porch (#/side-lanes); GROUND's rows; HARBOR's tail on the operator's hands; DreamDOM's train D on its own files.

''What moved down'' (2026-10-31): the keystone's `U2` to `U6` sit behind the hearth and wave 0; the spine's and the floor's rows sit behind waves A and B; the re-cut's wave C sits behind its gates; DreamDOM's next phases yield a slot to the hearth when both want one. Nothing left the road: each row stands in the stride that holds it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/cold-start`>>

!! Cold start ~ what a cold session reads, runs and obeys

!!! Read, in this order
# <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/noosphere-boot`>>, the boot seed, which every host seats whole at turn 1 by reference (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/seed-seat`>>); then this carrier, top to bottom.
# <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths`>> before the first commit: which session holds which ground, and the commit isolation that guards hands inside one session.
# <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/working-procedures`>> before the first test or wire: the red-first procedure in eight moves, the ladder for wiring a module that stands unreached, the instruments that read green while they lie, and the working agreements. Before any grammar work, <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/grammar-watch`>>.
# Once the memory stage stands (`L13`), its open-row index on the daemon wiki, before any file in the controller's scratch.
# Bring every ruling #/open names to the operator before a lane builds against it.

!!! Instruments ~ run each fresh before trusting a reading
* ''Branch'': `feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5`. `git rev-list --count origin/feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5..HEAD` reads the unpushed commits. The controlling session pushes only a head whose train read green over lanes whose CI and held-out read green, and only while no CI run stands in flight (`git push origin <sha>:refs/heads/feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5`); a lane pushes its own lane branch alone. Green has three readings: `node tools/ci-verdict.mjs --head <sha>` reads the branch's full CI as green, red or unread (a detached worktree names `--branch feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5`; the tool refuses a detached head without one), and prints beside it the held-out container drivers: the run that names the head, or else the newest run the branch holds, with that run's head, the commits between and each driver's result. A head no held-out run measured reads unread there, never green; the held-out reading says and never moves the exit code. Never a lane's to move, commit, stash or revert: `.worktrees/`, `.claude/worktrees/`, the `mempalace` submodule pointer, and the `VSCode-TW5-Syntax` pointer, which the operator pins at the released grammar while the grammar's own head stands ahead of that pin.
* ''Unit suites'': each package's own, under a scratch `LAR_ROOT` with `LAR_BAGS` unset; `node --test tools/*.test.mjs` for the tools. An import failure reads as zero tests, so read the file count beside the test count. `pnpm -r test` skips the root `tests/` directory by design.
* ''Typecheck'': `tools/typecheck-witness.sh`. Typecheck reads source and the binary reads `dist`, so run the built binary after any change that crosses packages.
* ''Witness fleet'': `bash tools/witness-all.sh`, the whole fleet, one run at a time through the battery lock, by a lane in its batch battery and again by the controller's train; a hand-picked subset reads greener than the tree. It holds out the witnesses its own `HELD_OUT` table names (the docker, browser and tree-clean ones). Capture its exit code directly, never through a pipe; `git status --porcelain` reads identical before and after a sound run.
* ''E2E'': every set rides CI (the lane branch's own `ci.yml` E2E job; `E2E_SETS` names the sets and the merge gate checks each set's union). `node tools/e2e-shards.mjs push <lane>` serves a bisect. A local run of one file proves that file alone.
* ''CI'' (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`): `gh run list --branch feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5` names the run for the pushed head; a later commit waits for the next push. `held-out.yml` runs on every push to a feature branch and to `main`, weekly, and by hand (`gh workflow run held-out.yml --ref <branch>`). It builds the product image once, runs one hosted job per cell (`node tools/held-out-cells.mjs cells` names them: each whole driver, and each scenario `tools/mesh-scenarios.sh --list` names), and its merge job prints one `HELD-OUT-DRIVER:` line per driver and one `HELD-OUT-VERIFY:` line. A cell that never answered reads red there by name. `node tools/held-out-steps.mjs <cell>` runs one cell's job on a desk, read off the workflow.
* ''The docker mesh'' (by hand): `crossing-witness`; `node tools/mesh-pins.mjs --up` stands the mesh pinned; `herm-mesh-witness` carries `node/alpha` across three hops; `mesh-scenarios nexus` (`tools/mesh-scenarios.sh --list` names every scenario and starts no build; `MESH_PROJECT` names the run's own compose project). The mesh publishes `MESH_PORT_*` on loopback, every node service requires `LARES_HARNESS_IMAGE`, and `tools/harness-image.sh` checks its digest. A hearth whose log carries a `[lararium] fatal:` line reads as not stood.
* ''Island doors'': `tools/capability-inventory-witness.sh` measures which vessel doors (fetch · rest · custody write · dyad) each island of the node and web assemblies reaches on the built tree, and every source call that spawns an island or attaches it a door, against the committed table `tools/capability-inventory.json`. The table measures reach, never a door's policy (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/the-harnesses`>>). A door a class reaches where the table says it did not, a new or extra seam, a seam that left or a new ask reads red by name; a deliberate change re-writes the table in the commit that makes it (`node tools/capability-inventory.mjs --write`). A browser wiki or session island reaches no door and meets the dyad refusal; the browser's daemon island reads `unmeasured`, since its spawn seam stands inside `openBrowserVessel`; a session island's view asks run through the pool core's own listener, which no row drives.
* ''The red register'': `packages/lararium-mesh/tests/unbuilt-laws.test.ts` declares each law the models imply and no code enforces; `tools/unbuilt-laws-witness.sh` reports a declared red that greened.
* ''Known environmental reds'': `lararium-tw5` `tests/syncer-back-parity.e2e.test.ts` (Chromium legs) and `tests/meme-routes.e2e.test.ts` fail where no browser or server socket stands; the browser suite reads `no tests` under a `HOME` that holds no Playwright Chromium.
* ''The operator's live `~/.lares` vessel'' holds an earlier shape that this build refuses. Never stand it here; it takes the fresh start, by hand (#/fresh-start).

!!! Steps
# `export LAR_ROOT=~/.lares-scratch/<lane>/root` before any in-tree boot or any `lares` command that touches the store. `LAR_ROOT` moves the store, the bags root and every projection mirror root; `LAR_BAGS` alone moves only the bags root, and the projection still writes the tracked `bags/` and `wikis/`. A staged e2e vessel takes its own scratch `LAR_ROOT` and no global `LAR_BAGS`. One `lares meme normalize|check` reads files and needs only a scratch `LAR_BAGS`.
# `LAR_ROOT` leaves these homes where they stand: `~/.mempalace` (the destructive `mempalace repave` among its readers), `~/.venv`, `~/.local/bin`, and the harness homes `~/.claude`, `~/.codex`, `~/.copilot`. `lares home` writes those homes under any `LAR_ROOT`, so a lane never runs `lares home tend`; `lares vessel` writes the scratch root alone.
# Build: `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` only where a lead approved an install, then `pnpm -r build`, then `pnpm --filter @lararium/tw5 build:tw5-vendor` (the TW5 core blob that `head-parity` and the plugin smoke need), then `node tools/stamp-build.mjs`. A fresh `git worktree` holds empty submodules; populate them before building there.
# ''A founding wants a human route on a TTY.'' With no TTY it refuses `CredentialTtyRefusal`, and no flag or environment variable carries a credential. A harness founds through the test door: `tools/test-scratch-marker.mjs` stages the root, `tools/seed-test-custody.mjs` names the published test route, then `lares vessel found --skip-build`; `tools/unlock-test-custody.mjs` opens a marked scratch root. An agent may read vessel state and hand the operator the next founding command as text; the operator performs each founding and custody act. A walk that meets `CredentialTtyRefusal` routes through this test door, and never through a terminal driver that types a credential; a walk brief names the door.
# ''Every boot rests at the floor.'' A verb that needs sovereign state refuses at a standing floor and names `lares vault unlock`. A harness that reads the first `gate key:` line of a log reads the carriage relay's key where a relay stands.
# ''The container'': `tools/lares-container.sh` offers `up`, `down`, `found` and `logs` (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor`>>). ''The browser surface'': `tools/browser-ci.sh` over `docker-compose.browser-ci.yml`, one project and image tag per builder (`BROWSER_CI_PROJECT`, `BROWSER_CI_IMAGE`).
# The tw5 plugin bundle (`packages/lararium-tw5/plugins/lares-memetic-wikitext.json`, `plugins/standalone/*`, `src/plugin-tiddler.generated.ts`) rides in git and must equal a fresh build: rebuild after any tw5 source or tiddler change and commit it with the change. CI's `build-freshness` job checks it. `genesis/` carries that bundle, the boot shadows and the engine blob by address, and the wiki inside a founded vessel runs the bytes genesis carries; a bake (`pnpm --filter @lararium/node build:genesis`) lands as its own labelled `GENESIS RE-BAKE` commit with the CAS change, and a vessel founded on the earlier seed re-founds. `tools/genesis-parity-witness.sh` reads the gap: a bundle that runs ahead of genesis reads red until a bake closes it or a row in `genesis/unbaked.json` declares it.
# `lares-cli` `tests/pour-repave.test.ts` needs the dev-root marker (`LAR_DEV_REPO_ROOT=1` or the ignored `lar-dev-root.json`).
# Crew: <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mixed-model-swarm`>> holds the seats, shapes, seat law, battery and lanes; the solo chain runs beside as many side lanes as hold disjoint files. A lead copies its resume sheet out of the session scratch directory after each commit: a restart empties that directory.

!!! The standing law that bites ~ read the carrier before arguing with it
* ''The frame stands as canon'': DOCTYPE, the SOH bearing, STX, `toml meta` first in the body, the body, ETX with its `ni:///sha-256;…` check, EOT. One span per carrier; a spelling outside the frame grammar tears the frame (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/memetic-wikitext-framing`>>).
* ''The frame's `?` and the gate's unechoed key stay distinct.'' `from="?"` declares an unresolved bearing at the grammar layer; the gate withholds a known key at the transport layer. Their one shared law reads at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/system-pattern-integrities#/threshold-pattern-integrities`>>.
* ''Ingest decides from a parse and a verdict.'' The deserializer stands as the pure meme-to-tiddlers function; `verdict()` (Match · Stale · Absent · Torn · Bare) lives in `@lararium/memetic-frame`; checks live in `carrier-check.ts`, repairs in normalize, policy at each door. A stale check on a human edit warns; a torn frame refuses; text with no frame reads as bare data, never a meme.
* ''Never refuse on the stock syncer path''; surface on the alert rail (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/syncer-back-parity`>>).
* ''The quoteblock floor fences at two grains'': the whole chunk inside the deserializer, and a child's body in place at the child gate (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/ahu#/quoteblock-floor`>>).
* ''An admit carries across a seal roll only through its roll anchors.'' The gate reads the presented admit against the carriage board's counted revokes and the kapae antigen, and folds no allow roster (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine`>>).
* ''No global now'' and ''the fence'': #/rulings.
* ''Grounds before wins'': rank work by what inherits shape from what, never by confidence in the next wire (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/working-procedures#/grounds-before-wins`>>).
* ''One holder per store, one Lararium per human'': a second holder meets `StoreHeld` across containers and namespaces (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor`>>).
* ''Fragments'': every `lar:` fragment roots at `#/…`, one raw `#`, `/` nests, `$` reserved (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/memetic-wikitext`>>).
* ''Grammar lives in the tiddlers'' (`packages/lararium-tw5/tiddlers/sigil-*.tid` generate `grammar-table.generated.ts`); sigil heads stay core ASCII; `aka` pins as information, `kanawai` pins as binding, `kahea` transcludes live.
* ''Early alpha'': no back-compat, no shims (one ruled exception: `G47`'s three sensorium placeholders), no legacy recovery; a stale thing aligns to the current frame and grammar, and a third human's arrival ends that line (#/rulings, the fence) (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/wild-bytes`>>). Derive, never enumerate. One function, one relation.
* ''A registry compiled about subjects refuses at any scope'' (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/registry-filter`>>).
* ''A relation's evidence keeps its type'' and ''a worker keeps its own storage scope'': <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/causal-island#/selected-contracts`>>.
* ''Shared tree'': never `git stash`; commit only your paths; never `--no-verify`; a spirit spawns no helper and never juggles another hand's uncommitted hunks; read an exit code directly; prove a zero with a known positive (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths#/law`>>).
* ''The node side routes nothing into the shore's files'' while a shore lane holds them: `packages/lararium-browser/**`, `packages/lararium-web/**`, `tw5-host-bridge.ts`, `tw5-projection.ts`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/rulings`>>

!! Rulings ~ the operator's, as approved

!!! The live test and the fence
* ''The live test runs with TWO operators on two machines from its first day'': the operator and the operator's daughter, in one house. The face-switch act, the cabal plane, the custody seal and the engine fold ALL land before it.
* ''The fence'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-16). Early alpha runs while one or two operators hold the mesh and ends when a third human's data and habits enter it; after that, data deserves a migration path. The second operator takes the seat beside the first as a QA tester who knows the mesh stays a sandcastle until then. As practice, not law, the destroy-class and shape-changing acts land before the second seat where they can: the engine re-mint and the genesis re-bake; the carrier and key-class changes; the Stable re-found (AID, charter, seat); every signed-shape change; every load-bearing rename.
* ''A working Lararium comes first'': Live Wiki Browser -><- Live Node, under manual QA on one machine. Sensorium work waits behind that milestone.
* ''A founding road carries its own succession.'' A reconstitution kit lets another competent operator stand a node and read its grammar; a rite held in one operator's head alone carries no succession.
* ''Face plurality stands as a day-one requirement.'' Each of the two humans wears at least three faces, each with a public Handle, on the first Nexus.

!!! The house as a container
* ''All CODE runs dockerized''; the host keeps only the hooks into the operator's environment (files and bags, session capture, the editor and CLI entry, custody).
* ''ONE Lararium per HUMAN, never per face.'' A human's faces keep their data together in one vessel (`PRIVATE-all, PUBLIC-one`); the recorded face-switch act carries plurality. No design assumes a container or a state mount per face.
* ''A SESSION wears the face; the vessel wears none'' (operator-ruled; quote license: //"a human might want a tab open as one Persona, and a tab open as another Persona... same vessel. Identity as relation."// and //"a CLI session wears its own face too … those act as ephemeral UX"//). A vessel holds its roots and one store. A session — a browser tab, a CLI run — contracts in as ONE persona, and several sessions may live at once over one vessel, each wearing its own. Every act carries exactly one face. Two faces live from one vessel never link on the wire: each session proves itself under its own face-scoped key, never under a key the vessel shares across them. The vessel-wide parts (custody, the daemon island) serve every session and let none read another's planes.
* ''Local-first first.'' Cloud "backup larariums" under other humans' delegated keys stand DEFERRED until the operator can deploy to a cloud surface. Nothing in the container or the face-switch work may foreclose a keyless ciphertext carrier later.
* ''The boundary'' (settled by the house under those rulings): one multi-arch image; `/workspace` mounts bags and wikis, `/state` mounts vessel homes and custody, never an image layer; the local socket stays INSIDE the container and the host CLI enters through `docker compose exec`; the app never receives the docker socket; mDNS offers unauthoritative LAN candidates and a pinned admission alone grants authority; DNS-01 wildcard TLS supplies browser secure contexts; a release manifest binds the image digest to the genesis, engine and grammar CIDs.

!!! No global now
* ''A wall clock never decides whether an act runs.'' Abandonment, revocation and expiry ride causal events; a timer serves only its holder's own liveness, and a device clock may sit under an adversary's hand. Web2.5 timers (TLS acceptance, OAuth tokens) sit at the edge of the causal island.
* ''Lapse rides a causal fence'': an epoch roll, a burn marker, or causal supersession at a verified relation fence. A replica missing that history reports an incomplete view, never "expired"; quiet never means lapse.

!!! The Stable state and its consequences
* ''Every Lararium holds a charter.'' One operator's charter reads as a SEED and two operators' seats in one charter read as a NEXUS (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus`>>). A household seed never grows into a Nexus; a Nexus founds fresh at threshold two of two. One "I" may hold several PersonaGroups. A Nexus grows by adding seats through its own rolls.
* ''A household seed serves its holder alone.'' A one-operator seed gives durable use without recruiting a mesh; useful local standing precedes every wider relation.
* ''Guests walk the household seed.'' Planes stay persona-scoped, so a guest sees its own documents, the public and infrastructure shelf, and every realm its worn face stands in; a face may stand in many, as a person belongs to many clubs, and a guest's other faces stand in none of them.
* ''Add-a-face (A).'' The daemon writes the public half; the terminal seals the recovery share; seating stays a separate act.
* ''Every leaf starts at the floor with its own root.'' A human may later JOIN a leaf to a PersonaGroup so its devices sync as one fleet; joining never takes the device's own root away.
* ''Founding persona zero belongs to standing'', never to a later verb. A capable vessel founds its household seed, so a "vessel with no charter" reads Mu.
* ''Stable folds into the re-found'' as its core.
* ''Forks report per board'': every presenter names every fork on the board it reads (the KERI watcher shape).

!!! Planes ~ cabal, realm, Nexus
* ''CABAL names the collective, REALM holds shared resources, NEXUS names the federated hardware mesh.'' The Nexus `@crossroads` board serves public-infrastructure docs and the Kahu Cabal keeps them. An operator's offering to a book club belongs in that club's realm, signed by the face worn there.
* ''Kahu drafts in the realm the charter derives''; publishing to `@crossroads` stands as a separate, recorded promotion act. A second Kahu realm gets made only for a genuinely different relation.
* ''Promotion approves the EXACT BYTES'' (hash, purpose, surface) under a threshold the charter sets, with a higher bar for charter, admission and trust-root changes. A CID, a clerk's edit or an active persona never stands in for collective approval.
* ''Steward signing.'' A scoped face signs acts that belong to one person; a transparent quorum Handle signs acts the collective owns; the root never appears publicly. A human wears MANY faces and many public Handles, each in many cabals, and signs a steward edge with the face or Handle worn IN THAT CABAL.
* ''Oracle answer'': one public answer per Nexus, signed by that Nexus's Kahu steward; a herm carries and presents it and signs nothing public. The engine floor rides as one shared content-addressed bake, and each answer names its floor CID (operator ruling).
* ''Herm tending'': tending may reach the herm's key; the house accepts that reach and names it (operator ruling).
* ''Introduction and admission'': a user introduces; an operator admits; the answer consumes the introduction (operator ruling).
* ''Names and counters'': a counter orders one writer's own emissions only, and the face lives at the session capability. The per-Nexus convergence secret and its keyring leave the model: nothing derives from one, the Stable founding act yields none, no seat act commits to one and no admission delivers one. (operator ruling; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/cap-tier#/key-delivery`>>).
* ''Kahu acts fall into four chartered classes'': ordinary (1-of-1); high-impact — charter, admission, trust root (3-of-5, or 2-of-3 for a small body); emergency (2-of-3, a narrow pre-authorized power reviewed by a later causal act); revocation (the threshold of the original act). A challenge appends to the record and may pause LOCAL reliance; it never halts the protocol. Admission of a Kahu or a Lamplighter always takes the seat quorum, and no ordinary single-seat class covers it; kapae and rolls take the quorum too; the user road rides its own bound (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus`>>).
* ''Renewal runs as three sequences, kept apart'' (the realm feed, a per-renewal causal position, the KEL), and lapse arrives as three distinct events: signed departure, recorded removal by relation-scoped kapae, renewal supersession.
* ''A cancel crossing a herm yields three receipts'': the caller's abandonment, the relay's delivery (transit only), the vessel's outcome. A caller timeout never counts as a cancel; eviction answers `retention-gap`.
* ''One face at a time names WHO ANSWERS for an act.'' The switch law reads in `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-policy#/one-face`.
* ''A session wears the face; the vessel wears none'' (operator ruling).

!!! Custody ~ the S key
* One S key, minted at the Stable founding, lives only inside LUKS-style keyslots, each a Clevis `sss` tree. Pin kinds: passphrase · paper (SLIP-39) · ember · tpm (one share only).
* ''EMBER'' names the Tang-shaped, per-relation device pin; it holds no share of S key. Only the blinded points cross on unlock.
* ''A backup route stands at every tier.'' Every keyslot tree, household or civic, keeps a human route (passphrase or paper); bind refuses a tree without one. Embers never stand as the only way in.
* ''The floor key keeps the vessel's public name''; the sealed custody root derives the veil and the sovereign caps. The veil change stays destroy-class.
* ''Unbind offers an S-key rotation loudly'', every time, never folded in silently.
* ''Locality law'': an unattended ember answers on the local link only, and only where the hearth holds a hardware freshness anchor the ember checks on each ask (#/rulings, the ember's key at rest); a relay pin wants an attending hand. The vessel rests at the floor and raises when t embers answer; the raise lapses by non-renewal, which reads as the raise's next causal boundary (#/rulings, how a raise lapses).
* ''HOT ⊥ COLD'': S key and keyhive state stay hot; the root, the recovery shares and the next-root seed unwrap per act.
* Measuring hardware x-only ECDH (phone enclaves) before trusting an enclave-held ember: approved, deferred.

!!! The hosted walk (F1)
* ''(c′) adopted.'' The HOST's gate reads and burns invites. The gate sorts every socket into same-operator · contracted · walker · stranger; under PRIVATE a stranger meets silence before any session frame. The guest carries its hosting grant; the hearth stores nothing about it. Invites mint by blind POPRF evaluation, so who-invited-whom stays non-computable. Revocation runs by kapae of the guest's leaf, by non-renewal, or by an epoch roll. Sybils meet tolerance, never detection.
* ''The inviter's face never signs an invite'': that signature would link the inviter at redemption. The hearth's blind evaluation carries the proof instead.
* ''V — vesting by class.'' A guest the HOST invites holds floor 1 at once; a guest a WALKER invites vests at the host's next roll. The roll reads as the household's consent to grow, and purpose labels separate the two classes without naming the minter. The built allowance carries the pre-answered dial of the user road's three; the operator's answer to a user's introduction stands owed, and until it lands a walker's token earns standing with no answer (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-invite`>>).
* ''W — the hearth carries a walker's own documents'' as sealed ciphertext (carry ⊥ read), under a quota per guest. The PersonaGroup holds the durable copy; hearth carriage stays a convenience, never a backup.
* ''W reclaim.'' Quota pressure alone triggers it; lapse reads as a RATIO against the guest's own rhythm between contacts, so the host's roll cadence cancels; the most-lapsed-for-itself goes first; notice on the guest's next contact comes before any discard. The ruling sets aside counting the host's rolls: it measures the host's diligence, and diligence confers no title.
* ''N — a node may walk.'' The sorter sorts by proven relation, never by device type.

!!! The cleanup of the hosted walk (B1)
* ''The worn face hosts and walks.'' No roster order picks anything; a vessel wearing no face hosts and walks nowhere. Before its first hosting act in a Nexus, a roll refuses when the hosting doc holds an act this face signed that no local state holds.
* ''A herm answers the knock.'' A charterless herm answers any proven peer that knocked (`ownPlacePosture`): a waystone by class, never a broken lararium. A lararium reads its charter, and an absent or torn charter reads PRIVATE.
* ''A boot follows `LAR_ROOT`.'' A vessel isolated under `LAR_ROOT` projects into that root, never into the tracked tree; the mirror roots never read `LAR_BAGS`, so the isolation instruction names `LAR_ROOT`.
* ''Compartments outrank admission control.'' A walker reads its own hearth's hosting doc, never the carriage board: the carrier set stays a contract ledger read by those who hold contracts.

!!! The first ceremony, and the acts around it (operator ruling)
* ''Guests'': the first ceremony runs with the host's own invites only. The hosted road builds whole before the ceremony (#/the-attractor): its doors stand built and stay shut on the day.
* ''Reach on the day'': LAN only. The public domain's Handle table comes later.
* ''At two seats no seat signs any Kahu act alone.''
* ''An operator's own herm'': an operator contracts their own herm with one signature, scoped to one place and one Nexus, and it falls with a kapae of the operator's admit.
* ''The hosting key'': the operator's seat signs ONE delegation to a hosting key; that key signs each hosting act and seeds each epoch. A session wearing any other face signs nothing for the hearth.
* ''The cold-key card'': the house format — 64 hex plus a checksum, in groups of four — printing its purpose, chair label, generation and a short charter fingerprint.
* ''The DNS credential'': the registrar's credential rests as two files on the first operator's always-on node, outside both mounts, read by the one-shot issuer. That node issues one wildcard per machine, and the second machine receives its certificate by hand.
* ''A browser leaf holds a passphrase or paper slot before it holds a persona root''; the mint refuses without one.
* ''No recovery contacts on the day'': each face starts one-of-one on its own paper, and enrolment comes later by one typed first-enrolment event the face's own current key signs on its own log.
* ''Shore saves, as built'': a rename onto a standing title proceeds as an ordinary write at field grain; a tombstone moved into a bag that lacks the title refuses unless the hand asks to shadow.
* ''A seat gets ADDED, never replaced.'' The long-span act adds a holder to BOTH cabals — a Kahu seat and a Lamplighter contract — and the founders keep their chairs; one founding Kahu may hold seats across many Nexuses. The road proves the roll that adds a seat, and the new holder's carriage contract, first. A passed chair stays a possible act. A Kahu who works far from the other seats needs a co-signature for every quorum act, so the partial-and-cosign door carries a partial act across a relay and back with no clock.
* ''Per-Nexus identifiers'': an admit names a per-Nexus identifier, a random leaf no other Nexus can link. The face's lineage may re-issue it by a signed hand-over the gate verifies, and per-Nexus keys mint at random, never from the persona seed.
* ''Every boot rests at the floor until a human unlocks'' (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor`>>). At the floor every fleet lane stays shut, and the custody write door, the rest door and the dyad door answer the daemon island alone. A bounded ember stays design input: it raises to SERVING alone, never signs or admits, runs on a count and never a clock, needs the device's own unlock plus a second signal, records each raise, and any other device of the human removes it.

!!! The models, each in its own carrier (operator rulings, approved as presented)
* ''Full models land early'': #/the-attractor.
* ''One roadmap'': this carrier. Standing procedures rest at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/working-procedures`>>, and the grammar watch at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/grammar-watch`>>.
* ''The sealed store'': cleartext lives in the live wiki and the operator's projected files alone. Private bytes take randomized authenticated sealing addressed over the ciphertext; the private tier takes no convergent sealing, and no opener returns unauthenticated bytes. A content key carries a version and rolls at a causal removal act. History at admission must have stood declared when written, and one logical bag name carries its key-roll epochs (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store`>>).
* ''The backup bundle'': a bundle holds ONE face, sealed under a route the person holds, and carries that face's root with its own lines and no key of any seat. Its memory rides as content keys, sealed inside the key island: a bundle opens every surviving value ever sealed under a version it carries, values written after its cut included, and no version minted later. A restore runs as a recovery act that takes a fresh veil and a new seat, and one face on a new device reads as the same audience for the versions that face already held (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody`>>).
* ''Removal's reach'': four edges take a marker (dyad, vouch, member, carriage). A vouch edge answers to its voucher and to a chartered quorum; a seal roll carries every shadow; a standing shadow reaches every gate and drives a key roll; a contested tie keeps the shadow up; mutual removal ends in an explicit fork (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/four-edges`>>).
* ''The PersonaGroup stands as a Keyhive group'', so a seat's revocation rolls the key of every bag the group reaches (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/keys`>>; a fresh group stays owed, `K12` of #/stride-1-spine).
* ''The hands'': every hand opens a session. A tasked spirit comes down to an AI, a Mask and the vessel's seat, attended or standing; the vessel's own key serves as a spirit's system identity; a Mask counts as no pet-name. The door carries and the hand authorizes, over one verb table, and a standing seat never signs an act that changes who holds authority (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-hands`>>).
* ''Sealed-row acts ride the custody door'', never a store verb whose output lands in the daemon's document in the clear (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape`>>).
* ''Gate keys come in three tiers'': a floor route's keys derive from the floor key, and a sovereign route's from the sealed custody root (operator ruling); the serving route's bundle derives from SK, a third tier that leaves those two untouched (#/rulings, the ember's serving gate). A faceless place takes a fourth arm at the gate, the place arm, and a session opens before its certificate. The floor key rests outside the vessel key, and the face proof names what a holder of the disk without the passphrase reaches and never reaches (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange`>>).
* ''The widget render boundary stands withdrawn'', and the parser carries recovery (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/graceful-parsing`>>).

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-10-16 ~ approved as the controller's story leans
* ''A joined device reads the hearth's corpus and keeps its own.'' A joinee mounts the hearth's `@lararium` corpus read-only beside its own, and its writes stay in its own; the oracle plane (the hearth's catalog, daemon doc and faces' planes) never crosses (`device-admit.ts#admitCanonDocs`). Row `W21` carries the build.
* ''The bag plane hides its creator before strangers carry it.'' The carried bag event's creator identifier in the clear gets measured red-first and ranked before wave three: ahead of anything that lets a third human's data cross a herm. Row `W25`.
* ''An agent's dissent drafts; a person's ask edits'' (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract`>> #/the-hands): an agent never overrides a person's edit, and a person's direct ask to edit a tiddler makes the edit the person's own act, bounded by the ask and the asker's authority.
* ''The system wikis release; every other wiki reads the floor'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-16). Only each Nexus's Kahu cabal writes the `lares` and `lararium` wikis and bags live. Every other wiki, and every other hand, mounts them read-only by CID: the latest release that cabal approved. The release rides the Nexus's crossroads board as its public answer, naming the floor CID under the Kahu steward's seal. The cabal approves the exact bytes under its charter's threshold, and a herm carries the answer without signing it. Every wiki mounts that floor through `@oracle`, which never writes. A vessel moves its floor pin only when it accepts a newer signed release. With no Nexus speaking, it stands on its last accepted floor, or on genesis. A face's wikis follow the floor of the Nexus that face stands in, so one vessel may hold two floors and never ties its faces together. An edit aimed at the floor copies up into the wiki's own working layer, and an offering back travels as a draft into the cabal's realm. A lone hearth is a Nexus of one, so its operator stands as its own Kahu. Row `W26` carries the build.
* DreamDOM's ruled questions stand in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom`>> #/open-rulings.

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-10-18
* ''Each browser tab asks for its own unlock.'' A tab that attaches to the shared holder opens the keystore by its own ask of the person; the holder carries no unlock between tabs. The reason reads off the session law: a session wears its own face, so each tab answers for its own opening (#/rulings, a session wears the face). The ask admits a tab to its face's open hive and derives no key: the holder keeps a face's keys while some session of that face stands unlocked, and the last to close locks it (operator, 2026-10-18; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026`>> #/what-both-faces-hold). Row `B1` carries the build.
* ''A face's secrets never rest clear, even at a first join'' (operator, 2026-10-18, approving the house lean). A fresh device's fleet join binds a passphrase or paper slot before the join lands, since every face secret refuses to rest outside the slot tree. Row `B1`.
* ''On the node, the boot marks the unlock and the session marks the face'' (operator, 2026-10-18, approving the house reading). The per-tab ask binds browser tabs; a node unlocks once per boot through its custody door, and every CLI run, MCP seat and watcher proves its own face at the gate without asking again (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026`>> #/the-node-scope).
* ''The last tab closes, and the browser rests at its floor'' (operator, 2026-10-18, approving the house lean). The unlock lives in memory alone (the browser vessel's own worker, never the page: #/rulings, a page never stands as a vessel), so the browser vessel's sovereign composition ends with its last tab and the next tab asks again. Row `B1`.
* ''One Keyhive identity per PersonaGroup, none above them'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18: //"it's the only way to retain control over faces that don't know about each other"//). Each face's PersonaGroup owns its Keyhive identity, its event store, its registry, its archive and its content-key seat; no vessel-wide identity registers a face's bags, delegates them, or holds a face's group. Two faces of one vessel then never link on the wire, a removal from one face rolls that face's bags alone, and a bundle restores one face and nothing of another. Today the vessel's own identity registers every bag and delegates each face's bags through itself, and the vessel and every face share one event store and one content-key holder (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/keyhive-per-persona-group-2026`>>). House readings under the operator's confidence, each naming what flips it: a herm keeps a floor-only transport key, behind a red test that it can neither author, resolve nor observe a face's capabilities, and holds no Keyhive identity if that test fails; several face hives share one process only after a substrate probe reads them isolated, and each face's hive runs in its own worker otherwise; co-mingled history migrates by fresh identities and an old mixed archive refuses by name, with no bridge; a founder that cannot leave its group leaves by a fresh PersonaGroup. Row `K15` carries the build, folding `W25` and `K12`; `W26`'s slice 3 waits on its face context.
* ''A cabal shares through the faces its members choose'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18, on the Teshima Family Council story). Kalani founds a private family council; Leilani, already standing on the DreamNet with several faces, chooses which of them join it, and a face she keeps back never learns the council exists. The council's hive reads as a Keyhive group with no key of its own: its members act through their own faces' identities, so nothing stands above them, and a presentation reads valid against the current set without naming which member made it (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/identity-classes`>> #/the-handle-chain). Any current member's face promotes into the council's canon; a graft takes the threshold; a face leaves by its own act, and another's removal rides `kapae` at the threshold or a fork that omits the captor (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm`>> #/the-unswept-corner); members read the owner-set as faces, never as humans. A private council declares no heraldry and lives in its members' address books under pet-names (cabal-realm #/heraldry). Row `K16` carries the story as `K15`'s acceptance.
* ''The asker seals'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19, F6's custody fork). Once the browser holder owns the Repo and the daemon kernel, it routes each sealed write to the admitted tab whose session caused it, else to any admitted, unlocked tab of that write's face; with no awake admitted tab, the write waits, named `seal-awaits-session`, never silent and never decided by a clock. No key material leaves a tab, and each sealer holds the row-class key the occupied carrier's overwrite proof opens. A SharedWorker carries no promise to run while its tabs freeze, and no `MessagePort` event reports a peer's death (round one, 2026-10-19), so a seal that waits names its wait.
* ''A face's custody rows seal under its own key'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19). Each PersonaGroup holds an independent random face custody key (FCK) that only its own slot tree opens (passphrase, paper or PRF), never wrapped under the vessel key, so a holder of the S key or of another face's unlock opens none of its rows. Face-private rows: each face's veil archive, face-key custody, recovery contact sets, face-provisional, the fleet-join address secret and record, a pending join bound to its target face, the per-face device shares, and each face's partition of the handle book and roster; the vessel Keyhive archive splits per PersonaGroup under `K15`. A face bundle carries that face's identity, archive, FCK recovery route and recovery contact set, and nothing of a sibling. Today every hot and cold custody row seals under one S key (`vault-carriers.ts`, `browser-custody.ts`), so face isolation reads as routing alone until this lands. Row `K15` carries the build, and `F6` slice three lands with its step 3. How a vessel-common act unlocks dissolves (next ruling).
* ''A shared act never makes a shared secret'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19; four ratcheted research rounds, <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/custody-per-face-2026`>>). Every act that touches more than one face sorts one of three ways: its private bytes DISSOLVE into the owning face's rows (D), its public bytes stand at the FLOOR by design (F), or it names a vessel SECRET (S) whose principal and opener the house names row by row. A mixed carrier splits before it sorts. The browser holds no vessel key: founding, fleet join, slot changes, roster, archives, handle book, dyads, restore and clear sort D or F, so each tab asks only its own face's unlock and public floor work asks nothing. Phones, installed web apps, probes, the CLI, MCP and watcher seats and a native shell hold no S. A tablet waking floor carries ciphertext and public rows unattended and decrypts no face's row. A container restarts at its floor and nothing unlocks it unattended. A guest island that owns a key store stands as its own vessel. A node holds S only for rows it names: the serving key has no row yet, so it guards nothing today. * ''The place signs by a scoped signer under a pre-rotated KEL'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19; a fifth research round, <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/custody-per-face-2026`>> #/the-place-signer). A node's place identity keeps a KEL whose inception commits the next key, and that next key rides the founding card beside the seed charter's cold next key, offline; the online place signer runs unattended for an enumerated allowlist of floor duties alone (the KEL's own events excepted, which the card's key signs), rests under the host's TPM-bound credential where the host offers one and an owner-only file elsewhere, and never rides an environment, an image or a log. A stolen online signer rotates away by the card; a lost card beside a stolen signer re-founds. The floor holds no custody key; it holds its own named place signer. The format change rides the founding bake (`X0`). The place KEL commits its next cold key on the founding card and rotates by that card (`W29`), and the seed census names every reach of the seed past the floor, K15's owed bonds among them (`tools/seed-census.mjs`).
* ''Custody and identity read by capability, never by host'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19: //"I consider node and browser isomorphic-by-intent. Eventually, a browser-vessel somewhere may grow comparable capabilities to a node vessel, and this pattern cascades outwards to all future vessels."//). Three rules hold on every vessel, present and planned: a vessel meets each face through that face's atom alone, its veil signing every dial, join, enrolment, sibling channel and daemon proof made on that face's behalf, never one device-wide key; a vessel holds a place signer exactly while it stands a floor, for the floor's allowlisted duties, under the pre-rotated place KEL; and a faceless vessel reading another's floor proves nothing private, reading the public shelf under the floor card's pin. A node's daemon auth, device-key signer, sibling enrolment and group-secret opener read the one vessel seed today (`open-node-vessel.ts`), and a browser's do the same (`open-browser-vessel.ts`); each is a bond owed, carried by `K15`.
* ''A page never stands as a vessel'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-29: //"Approve your leans"//, after the mirror-realm talk-story). Every page holds a SURFACE with a port onto a vessel, in every setup: a browser on a node's own box, an anon-device browser vessel, a fleet of equivalent vessels. The no-VM-on-the-main-thread law reads "no sovereign VM and no key on a surface" (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/lararium-canonical-model`>> #/the-laws), and the DreamDOM mirror, a camera carrying a whole engine and no truth, honors it (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom`>> #/open-rulings). Custody follows: the anon-device browser vessel keeps its opened key in its own worker, never in page memory (the per-tab ask stands, and the shared holder still carries no unlock); it seals its store through sealed-store steps 2 and 3; and it takes an unlock ceremony the surface cannot paint, the boundary sitting where the person types the secret (its shape stays research). The full mirror runs people's plugins only after those seal steps land for the browser store; no clear row stands under the full mirror, and the device key rests non-extractable. Browser persona roots already rest sealed once a slot binds (`browser-custody.ts`); only the place seed and veil archives may stand clear before a slot, and the opened tree key in page memory names the exposure this ruling moves.
* ''Identity enacts a bond'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19). Lineage stays within a root, and covenant joins roots: a root (a compartment seed, a vessel key, a face custody key, a charter seat, a content key) derives children for its own duties alone, and any identity spanning two roots is a bond, an act both ends sign that seats a fresh key of its own, resting with the end whose privacy it serves and lapsing by its own act; no closure holds a root (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/identity-enacts-relation`>> #/identity-as-bond, its seven laws and bond table).
* ''A face's device veil is its own random key'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19, re-reading the 2026-09-05 veil ruling). Each PersonaGroup's veil on a device mints at random when the device joins that face and rests under that face's FCK, so no device seed stands as an ancestor of any face's veil; it stays vessel-local, never presents, and reads mint-shaped to every observer. The group-secret opener follows it.
* ''Each realm stands under one charter, a seed's or a Nexus's'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18). A council that meets at one hearth stands under that hearth's seed charter, and its guests' chosen faces walk there with no Nexus founded; a council two hearths carry stands under the Nexus those two found fresh, since a Nexus names the operators' relationship (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae`>> #/edge-hands). A guest sees every realm its worn face stands in.
* ''A write lands on a working layer, and canon takes it by promotion'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18). A `LOAD` lands on a wiki's working slot alone; canon takes content only by the cap-gated promotion `MOVE` (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/wiki-layer-ontology`>>), and genesis and founding seed canon through their own named door. An act aimed at a wiki no island mounts asks the vessel to mount it, and refuses gracefully, naming the cause, when the mount cannot stand. Row `W27` carries the build.
* ''Every bag opens as its own wiki'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18). The floor recipe names the bag alone over `@oracle`, derived and never minted, and an absent record reads as that floor, never as "no such wiki"; a composed wiki's record, which a hand authors, lays several bags and plugin offerings beneath the wiki's own canon bag (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/wiki-layer-ontology`>> #/quine). No act enumerates bags to mint wikis. Row `W27` carries the build.
* ''One owner per registry; the floor reads by CID'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18: //"Architecture stands, leans stand"//). The `@oracle` document as a registry every vessel writes retires: one id carried one `lares` and one `lararium` pointer for the whole fleet, and a joined vessel's pointer replaced its hearth's, a shape a two-vessel probe and three shard runs measured. `@oracle` stands as the floor's read-face over a manifest CID, which a wiki mounts read-only by the pin its face accepted. Each registry carries only what its scope's owner writes: a vessel's bootstrap holds its own document pointers and its accepted floor pins; `@catalog` the operator's documents; the Kahu realm the live `lares` and `lararium` documents and their drafts; the crossroads board the Nexus's one public answer. The leans the ruling adopts: the Kahu realm holds the live system documents, with `bags/` projected on the Kahu's vessel alone; a one-seat founding lands a counted 1-of-1 Kahu-cabal admission for persona zero's group, so one predicate decides every hand's reach into system canon; the release answer, the Nexus's one public answer, names the Nexus AID, the charter epoch, the release before it and the floor manifest CID, sealed by the steward, with the promotion approval over the manifest's exact bytes riding attached; a vessel records an accepted release as a sealed hot row per (face, Nexus) that only a person's act moves; genesis bakes the first floor, a corpus CID in the seed. A signing domain of its own carries the release, `floor-release`, so no seal over another shape verifies as one (operator-approved, 2026-10-18). The shape and its slices stand at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/w26-floor-lane`>>; rows `W26`, `W21` and `P8` carry the build.

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-10-31
* ''Recovery contacts, custodians and the Handle'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"Approve recovery contact, and during this rewrite we should use Handle (& explain it) over 'name'."//). A person who holds a recovery key over one face's key history and signs its rotation reads as a ''recovery contact'', the field's own word for the role. A quorum of a face's recovery contacts rotates the face's root beneath its fixed KEL prefix, so the face keeps its ''Handle'': the public, self-certifying card a face publishes and others recognise by its own key, never by a registry and never by a pet-name (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/heraldry`>> #/ruling). A holder of one share of a root seed reads as a ''custodian'' (`CustodianTag` already says so). A custodian card returns memory, a recovery contact returns the Handle, and neither stands in for the other. ''Guardian'' keeps only its human sense (a child's legal guardian) and the fiction (the Lares as guardian spirits), and the Kahu keep their own name. Text a person reads says "your Handle" where it means what recovery restores, never "your name". The rename across canon, code and the CLI rides one lane, with a weld test before it.
* ''Two of three recovery contacts'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"2 of 3"//). A face enrols exactly three recovery contacts at threshold two: one lost contact leaves recovery standing, and no single contact can rotate the face alone. Enrolment refuses any other count or threshold by name. The face's own self-recovery digest counts as none of the three (operator, 2026-10-31, approving the house lean): it keeps its own road, a seed in hand rotating the face alone, so the quorum that can rotate a face never includes a key the person lost. Row `K18` carries the door.
* ''The memory-stage hearth comes first'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"I need a locally working node+browser joined (all vessels start at anon floors, so the full rites), so you all can stop using md in an untracked scratch dir & start using the daemon wiki as a 'memory-stage' (see neuroscience + Ai developments) for all design tracking and self improvement feedback loops."//). One operator's node and browser stand joined on one machine, each from its anon floor through the full rites, and the house tracks its design and runs its feedback loops on the daemon wiki in place of untracked scratch files. The rung leads the road (#/the-attractor, #/the-order); its rows read in #/stride-0-hearth. House reading: the stage runs on plain tiddlers, tags and one index view, with no embedding, no vector index and no sidecar, so the sensorium arc's deferral stands as written (#/the-road, off this road).
* ''The daemon wiki holds the memory stage, and the sensorium waits'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"Pono, we raise the wiki (and hold the sensorium work). That all reads pono intent for the `daemon` wiki itself (the other wiki-domains serve different intents)."//). The hearth stride raises the daemon wiki as the house's memory stage; no other wiki domain takes that intent. The sensorium re-stand stays held behind it.
* ''The working surface is the repo checkout'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"Approve (b), the working surface IS the SDM repo checkout after all (but this should be configurable for other operators or myself to do a 'non-dev install' later)."//). The operator's house mounts the development checkout as `/workspace`, so its disk projection writes the tracked `bags/` and the `wikis/` beside it and git holds the memory stage's history. `LARES_WORKSPACE` keeps the choice an operator's own: a non-dev install points it at a corpus directory of its own (`L16`). Test and lane boots keep their scratch `LAR_ROOT` and never touch this surface.
* ''Narration lives in the wiki, and the flat memory folds into it'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"Once the wiki sits live and all agents can write memes to it, query it using filter language, etc, then we need to enforce that for all 'self-narration design and talk story' that used to end up on disk (and note in the ~/.claude 'memory system' that always get loaded that we intend to o-takiage memories from that flat file store to the wiki, and the main memory file should point directly to the MCP capabilities on its first line."//). `L17` gives the wiki its filter, `L18` fences narration onto it, `L19` folds the flat memory store into it.
* ''A single operator's re-found reads idempotent by intent'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"As git holds all history & refounding for a single operator reads idempotent by intent (we can freely tear down identity & costs at any time), we need this goal added to the overall roadmap. Combine the road/roadmap & reprioritize with these adds."//). While one operator holds the mesh, a re-found tears identity down freely: git holds the history, so a teardown costs nothing git cannot restore, and the same rites land the same house shape again under fresh keys. A re-found runs the way the fresh start runs, by hand and reversible, never through a pave or wipe command (#/fresh-start). It narrows no standing ruling, and the house reads it beside them: the fence holds, so the shape cuts land before the second operator seats a key and a re-found stays cheap until her pins depend on it (#/rulings, the fence); the live test still runs with two operators from its first day; after `X0` a re-genesis costs every CID a second operator pins (#/stride-3-bake), while a single operator's identity re-found before her seat costs nothing; old shapes still burn, so a format cut re-founds and no source reads pre-cut bytes (#/rulings, the fresh start). `X0b`'s idempotent-by-intent pass sweeps the code a re-found replays, and the memory stage keys its rows so a re-pour reproduces them (`L9`).
* ''The test re-cut moves before the founding'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"Approve lifting the test re-cut ordering to before founding"//). This lifts the 2026-10-29 ruling that set the re-cut after the founding (#/rulings, the test refactor). Red-first commits, the train's merged-tree run, CI as the verdict and one Sol read per train stay. Train T carries it (#/stride-t-recut).
* ''The re-cut's house leans, approved whole'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"Approve your leans on all forks"//):
** the composer's stage-cut runs before the founding only as a named single-owner step, after boot characterization tests (`T12` after `T6`)
** the dead-export sweep burns the 53 names nothing references; the 16 barrel re-exported names wait on their own ruling (`T4`)
** wave A and the pure readers open as lane slots free, four lanes at most
** shared stages live in `lararium-mesh` (transport-free stages) and `lararium-tw5` (keel stages), with no new package
** a witness pins that the glass mirror's plugin set never holds a daemon-only title (`T15`)
** the verb providers' matrix shaping lifts into a daemon-only `*-vm` module, and sensorium state stays in py (`T14`)
** cut order: the pure readers first, the doors and boot cells after `u1`, the share inputs after `ralias`
* ''The cut sorts by capability'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"Browser vessels do use all these caps, and may enact a 'node-like role' later, so hodl that isomorphic-by-intent-and-cap-stack design."//). Dials, relays, face-gates, the share verdict and the membership holders read as ''cap-stack stages'' behind ports named by capability (serves a gate · dials · relays · carries for others), never as a node shell. Only the substrate binding (thread kind, store kind, socket server) stands per host. No stage, port or package carries a host name (#/rulings, custody and identity read by capability, never by host).
* ''Every cut candidate takes a full cold read first'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"Pono, I want the 'cut candidates' to get batched Sonnet-scout-spirit cold-readers in full before we even touch them."//). No lane opens on a cut row (`T4`, `T5`, `T9` to `T14`, `T16`, `T17`) until a Sonnet cold reader, batched by seam, has read its whole span, never by excerpt, and its report stands. The read names what each candidate does, the state it shares, its callers, the laws it touches and what the cut risks; a lane brief cites it.
* ''Each cut read collides with a second reader'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"collide each spirit's findings with another Sonnet cold reader that has internet research caps, then land all of their 2-wave researches into the meme."//). Six wave-one readers read the cut candidates in full by seam; six collision readers re-read each span, ruled every finding confirmed, refuted or narrowed, searched outside precedent, and named what wave one missed. The train's kit holds what survived both waves (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/test-recut-road`>> #/the-reads).
* ''Stryker runs in a sandbox probe'' (operator approval, 2026-10-31: //"Approve the Stryker 10.1.1 probe in a scratch worktree"//). `inPlace` stays false, local reporters only.

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-11-01
* ''Train Q, the kupono grammar cut'' (operator approval, 2026-10-31, every house lean with the chiasmus folded in; scheduled 2026-11-01). One lane re-cut the whole sigil grammar and every bag before the memory stage takes its first tiddlers: the bare arrow left every sigil for `from=` and `to=`, no `@` follows whitespace, every sigil value takes a backtick under a name, the turn frame folds into `set lares=` with `aim`, `steer` and `yield`, and the turn harvesters burned. Its rulings, the `steer` name, the `slot=` and `action=` names, a steer carrying no `from` (operator, at the seed's gate: //"I don't think from= is needed inside a chiasmus."//) and the seed's ceiling lifted past 40K (operator: //"We can exceed the 40K ceiling if needed, as we now deterministically load the boot seed via hooks."//) among them, live in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/grammar-watch#/the-kupono-cut`>>, with its one provisional clause on the turn frame. Landed, PR #149 (#/o-takiage).
* ''Before Q'' (operator rulings, 2026-11-01: //"Approve all this before Q"//, then //"Approve, hold the gh batching for later- enact all others now & flow in parallel fashion to the gh batching"//). The scratch fixture, the ten-job CI, the local merge queue and the native KDF landed ahead of ''Q'' (PRs #142, #145, #146, #147); the hosted merge queue's wiring, its trial and the span's ruleset wait on the operator's admin step and return as their own small lane, and ''Q'' opens without waiting on them. A local merge queue in the land tool (contract D) takes the hosted queue's model meanwhile (operator, 2026-11-01: //"I meant use their mental models locally to land our queue without the rebase waits."//). Local CI runners stay out (//"Reject local runners"//).
* ''The fast tier runs related tests'' (operator ruling, 2026-11-01: //"Approve the fast-tier lever"//). A lane's local fast tier runs each touched package's related tests, never its whole suite; whole suites, every e2e set and the slow witnesses run in the cloud (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mixed-model-swarm`>> #/the-battery).
* ''The fiction and the art take their own o-takiage'' (operator ruling, 2026-11-01). The RPG chapters and the concept art keep an o-takiage rite fitted to their own wiki domains, designed from the pono sweep, and the lares design laws never bind them by default. Row `L20`.
* ''Train R, block and inline sigils at parsing parity'' (operator approval, 2026-11-01: //"Approve 1 + 2a(3 floor) cleanup of inline vs block sigils to tw5 pono parsing parity"//). A block sigil owns its line, the seal rides the ETX foot as a named value, a sigil whose `>>` leaves text after it reads inline as TiddlyWiki splits it, and trailing text reads as a recovered node with a diagnostic, never silent. Two addons ride it (operator, 2026-11-01): every code fence in the boot seed names its grammar, so the syntax plugin colours it (//"code-fenced blocks in the boot seed should get the grammar marked explicitly"//), and a fence opens and closes with more ticks than the longest run inside it. It follows ''Q'' as its own train and keeps the before-`L4` property (#/the-trains, R).
* ''The legacy checks burn once the trains land'' (operator rulings, 2026-11-01: //"once this train goes through, we should o-takiage even those 'legacy checks' to get back to pono early-alpha evergreen state"//, then //"hold to alpha evergreen & after all this lands o-takiage the checks that deny stale/legacy forms. Especially the file-path tiddler field wards (pono to purge them, but it would then block legit user choices if kept)."//). A check that refuses only an old spelling burns; a law guarding a live invariant keeps. `file-path` retired as the house's meta field in ''Q'', so the name returns to user space as a plain field, and its deserializer deny, its `meme check` red and its witness leg burn with the rest. `uri-path` keeps (operator: //"`uri-path` serves the wiki as a toml-tiddler-field, keep that."//). A Sonnet cold reader, then a blind Opus security reader, sort the checks before the lane burns any (#/side-lanes, the legacy-check pyre).
* ''The deps move before the founding'' (operator rulings, 2026-11-01: //"I want to collide those old holds"//, then //"Approve both leans pending the trials, flow on"//). The engine holds read as claims under test: Automerge moves to 3.5.0 if its trial reads green, Keyhive to 0.6.0 as the one port before the founding if its trial shows the CGKA standing from the Archive and the port runs one or two days, else 0.3 holds; `vitest` 5.0.5 and the hygiene bumps ride the same lane. Subduction and Onomancy stay out, and a TiddlyWiki re-pin stays an epoch act (#/side-lanes, the deps before the founding).

!!! House rulings under the operator's delegation ~ the questions the operator delegated, each with what flips it
The operator delegated these to the house: each stands as a house ruling the operator may overturn, its law lives in the carrier it names, and its build rides the stride rows #/the-loops indexes.
* ''The ember's key at rest'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A separate serving key (SK), the fifth custody class `serving`, wrapped by the vessel key and by ember slots, seals the serving rows alone, and the serving tier holds no minting secret: a walker's grant checks under a public half, a shape the hosted road's re-cut lands before the ember's walker arm. An unattended answer (any slot that answers without a person's confirm on the ember) needs a freshness anchor a disk copy cannot carry, a hardware-sealed hearth key whose attestation the ember checks on each ask; without one every ember slot answers only on a person's confirm, threshold two buys a second confirm and never an unattended raise, and after any counted removal a bare deny-only `sk-rotation-owed` mark refuses every ask until SK rolls, a mark a rolled-back disk copy can omit. ''For the operator'': a hearth that holds no hardware anchor raises only on a person's confirm. The reason: a key that opens serving state opens nothing beside it, and a copied disk replays any freshness it carries; the ruling flips on a threat review showing the serving rows hold nothing a thief can use even before the grant re-cut (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table`>>; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember`>>).
* ''The ember's serving gate'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A third gate tier, the serving route, takes its bundle from SK, and the floor and sovereign tiers stand untouched; a pin moves only by a signed causal event or a verified key-history step, and no quiet period ages a pin out. The reason: a dialer that pinned the sovereign tier never mistakes a serving hearth for an unlocked one. The ruling flips on proof that a gate pin decides no authority anywhere (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange`>>).
* ''How a raise lapses'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A raise lives one boot and lapses at the next causal boundary (a lock, a restart, a human unlock, a counted removal), and that boundary IS the expiry the waking floor requires; no timer and no heartbeat ends a standing, and the count spends at the raise ledger's durable commit before SERVING composes, so a crash before the commit spends nothing and serves nothing and a crash after it burns the raise. The reason: quiet never tells a slow device from a gone one; the residue: a rolled-back ledger on a disk copy restores spent raises. The ruling flips on a causal renewal event every reader receives that also survives the ember's loss (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape`>>).
* ''A removal no reader can keep'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). The based removal refuses by name where no parallel-drafts reader keeps the concurrent words, or every reader failed, and the store authors nothing; the refusal carries a durable conflict handle (the document, the removal's request id, the conflicting change and head identities, never the hand's text), so a hand whose editor went offline finds the same conflict on its return. The reason: a draft the store wrote would choose a bag, a tier and a steward no hand named. The ruling flips on an already-authorized destination for such words that keeps provenance and tier without the store choosing either (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/automerge-doc-store`>>).
* ''The outbox at rest'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Each outbox parcel seals under randomized authenticated encryption with a stable outbox key the vessel key wraps and an unlock hands the daemon island, the way content keys reach it, and rests under a random name with its routing inside the sealed body, so a locked disk names no reader; the `sealed-outbox` row leaves `floor-plain` for a sealed class. Before a parcel's atomic commit nothing stands sendable, after it the parcel re-opens and retries at the next unlock, and after a ship and before the local delete the receiver folds a repeat idempotently. The reason: a reader digest on a stolen disk names a relationship; no flip stands expected, since the ruling closes a residue (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table`>>).
* ''The herm relay leg'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). The ask rides the sealed sibling session under that session's seal with a tagged inner frame, and the delivery receipt stays a bare session note good for the asker alone, bound to a unique request id and the session's causal position, attesting transit and never execution; reach narrows to one PersonaGroup, and the leg lands with `W9`. The reason: the fewest new shapes, and no signing domain minted for a transport fact. The ruling flips on a concrete need for a third party to audit relay delivery after the asker vanishes (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/receipt-ledger#/the-relay-leg`>>).
* ''The disk watcher's reach'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A pending removal settles with no person present only under a standing instruction the operator signs by a human route, naming one bag and a bounded class of removals (a path pattern and a per-wave count ceiling): the watcher executes within it, every settled removal lands in the audit with the instruction it rode, and `lares shore restore` still answers it. An automated seat never signs one, a standoff (disk and records differ) never settles without a person, and a mass vanish past the instruction's ceiling stays pending for a person. The reason: a person who answered in writing for exactly that class has answered, and a count alone proves no intent; the ruling flips on a single incident where an in-scope instruction deleted what a person meant to keep (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection#/projection-law`>>).
* ''The restore's key history'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A restored face stands PROVISIONAL until at least one party to its relation (a sibling device of the same human, a seated chair of its Nexus, or a recovery contact) shows its own log view and that view agrees with the restore's head; while provisional the face reads, recovers and serves its own content and signs no authority-changing act, the restore prints which head it read and from where, and the provisional state names who could confirm it. Where every such party stays unreachable, the recovery contacts' path (the impersonation quorum) answers, never the local read alone. The reason: no vessel reads a head it never received, and the house keeps no registry of heads; no flip stands, and the residue stays stated: one agreeing party may itself sit stale (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore`>>).
* ''The Offering Antigen scope'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A signed Offering act carries a stable scope, the joint Nexus AID its signed inception mints, beside `sealEpochCid` as the authorizing epoch. The receiver resolves its own charter scope, verifies the act's epoch inside that scope's locally held lineage, checks the quorum at that exact epoch, and only then folds; an act whose scope differs stays as foreign evidence outside the `kapae` and `un_kapae` fold, and an incomplete lineage reads `unavailable`. The joint inception image commits to the offer fingerprint and to both founders' fresh cold successor commitments, and a successor commitment a vault already used refuses, so the AID names one founding statement and two foundings by one roster never alias. The reason: a neighbour's condemnation copied onto another board reads as testimony, never as that board's verdict. The ruling flips on a case where two foundings by one roster must read as one Nexus (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/offering-antigen-scope-decision-record`>>).
* ''The fork-resolve act'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A fork stays a fork, and every reader names the branch it follows; no clock, no longest chain and no third-party quorum picks a branch. Two acts resolve one. ABANDON: a seal record a branch's own quorum signs at its head, closing that branch for every reader who holds it, erasing nothing and touching no sibling. REUNION: one canonical image naming the scope AID, both held heads sorted, and the new seats, threshold and successor commitments, signed by each branch's quorum at its head over identical bytes; the verifier proves both heads inside the same AID's lineages from a common ancestor, and the record becomes the common head. A seal record's predecessors form a sorted set of one or two, built before the fence; until the reunion verifier lands, a two-predecessor record refuses `REUNION_UNBUILT`. The reason: a split household reunites only by a paper both halves sign. The ruling flips on a fork that neither branch's quorum can still sign for.
* ''The re-seal proof'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). When a content key rolls at a counted removal, the face session that writes each replacement signs one causal re-seal record per replacement: the bag locator, the old and new key-version ids, the old and new ciphertext addresses, the logical entry it replaces and the removal act that authorized the roll. The removal quorum signs the roll, never each body. A reader with partial history verifies the signature and every binding it holds, and reports a missing parent as unverified, never as complete. The format lands with sealed-store step three, before the fence. The reason: a phone offline through the roll needs to know which photo replaced which, and whose hand re-sealed it. The ruling flips on proof that the key-version record, manifests and checkpoints already show every replacement and omission to both kinds of reader (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store`>>).
* ''Recovery contact enrolment's shape'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Each face founds one-of-one with no recovery contacts, and its log's verifier knows a typed first-enrolment event from the start: the face's own current key signs it, it commits the recovery contact set and threshold, and the face's prefix stays as it stands; every later change to the set takes the recovery contacts' authority. The event shape and its verifier land before the fence, so an enrolment never re-founds a face. The reason: a face founded alone keeps its Handle's history when recovery contacts join a year later, and no third hand holds standing over a one-of-one face. The ruling flips on an existing event kind that already permits that first, face-key-signed transition. The count stands ruled at two of three recovery contacts (#/rulings, 2026-10-31); request authentication, the veto, death and succession stay open (#/open).
* ''Engine cadence'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Cadence keeps its own lineage and threshold, apart from every seat key. A Nexus's joint founding cites one cadence inception that names each founder's hearth, the device that bears the reboot, as a holder; a household seed carries none, since its one holder bears its reboots. Each hearth mints its cadence key at random on the device, never derived from serving or floor material, and keeps it, with its next-key commitment, as a `cold` row under the vessel key that unwraps per act; no sixth custody class and no extra card at the founding. A holder may later rotate onto a cold card by its own choice. Each island pins one engine epoch, and one daemon hosts two epochs only as two separately pinned islands. The reason: the people who bear a reboot decide it, and a seat-holder who runs no hardware forces no one's restart. The ruling flips on a cadence act that must run unattended where a per-act unwrap cannot (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/contract`>>).
* ''A realm's quorum and its boards'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Each realm stands under ONE Nexus, which the vessel's first act there names and its edge ledger keeps; every reader takes the realm's quorum roster off that Nexus's charter, never off its own primary one; and the realm's boards key on the realm's own id, so a removal's record stays among its parties. The reason: authority comes from the governing charter while the record addresses the relation it affects. The ruling flips on a realm-native charter model with its own seat creation, migration and conflict rule (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/edge-hands`>>).
* ''A restore after recovery contact enrolment'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). A face's own bundle restores after it enrols recovery contacts, so long as the face's log still holds the set its own root enrolled by the typed first event and no later event moved it; a recovery contact rotation that moves the set cuts every earlier bundle off. The restored face stands provisional as the restore's key-history ruling holds. The reason: enrolling recovery contacts must never silently kill a person's own backups, and the recovery contacts' rotation, not the enrolment, cuts off a stolen bundle. The ruling flips on a provisional gate that cannot stop a restored face from signing an authority-changing act before a party confirms it (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore`>>).
* ''Paper restores a root, never memory'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). The split-root cards rebuild the persona root alone; content keys stay random and wrap under the vessel key or a bundle's own key, and no key the root derives opens memory. A person recovers memory through a bundle, under the route that bundle seals. The reason: a root card that opened memory would turn every share holder and every thief of the paper into a reader of the face's private bytes. The ruling flips on an explicit choice that root-share holders read memory, with the custody that choice costs stated.
* ''A restore as persona zero'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). A restore onto a fresh hearth runs through the founding act: the hearth founds a fresh one-seat seed charter, and the restored face stands as its persona zero and holds its new chair. A bundle's old chair claim carries no authority on the new hearth, since a bundle carries no seat key. A founding that stopped part way resumes only its own persona-zero root and its own seed charter, and refuses a root that differs from the restored one rather than minting over it. The reason: a person who lost their one machine meets their own face, never a second one. The ruling flips on a rule that a lost hearth's chair must survive, which would make a bundle carry seat authority it excludes.
* ''A roll waits on its prior anchor'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-15). A seal roll refuses by name while the prior roll stands with no anchor, so no Nexus stacks a roll over a step its readers cannot yet carry across a removal. The reason: an admit carries across a seal roll only through its roll anchors, and a roll laid over an anchorless one strands every admit between them.
* ''The working address'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). A face's working document takes its id from a dedicated face address secret: 32 random bytes minted once per face, sealed on each of that face's vessels, delivered to a joining vessel by the fleet-join door, and moved only by a deliberate rotation act that opens a fresh working document; neither a per-Nexus key's re-issue nor a vessel key touches it. The id reads as the first 16 bytes of HMAC-SHA-256 keyed by that secret over the length-prefixed domain tag `lar/working-address/v1`, the wiki relation's stable id (the face's `wikiId`, never a recipe CID, a composition fingerprint or a label, so a recipe edit or a rename moves nothing), and the slot kind in its lowercase spelling (`draft`, `working` or `personal`; `temp` holds no document). Each working document starts empty, with no initial change, so two offline vessels of one face converge on one history; a vessel's first write lands as an ordinary change. The reason: two devices of one face open one notebook offline and sync into one, while a keyed id lets no observer link two faces or read the secret. The ruling flips on a face identity contract from train N that already names shared face-secret bytes with the same custody and rotation.
* ''The admit's gate pin'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). The admit carriage and fleet-join answer sign a validated full `GateBundlePin` for the hearth's sovereign sync route at the named Nexus, read by `readGateBundlePin`; its digest derives from that object. Dials index by (hearth, Nexus, route, tier). Floor routes receive their own full pins through an authenticated relation or a carried signed pin set; an announcement never authenticates its own first pin. The reason: a digest alone gives an offline dialer no trusted route or key. The ruling flips on an equally authenticated digest-to-full-pin carrier with an unambiguous route and offline first contact.
* ''Frames after open'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). A versioned sealed application header carries kind (`session`, `sync`, or a named control kind), message id, part index and count, total length, then bytes, only after the gate seat opens. Gate tags `0x01`–`0x13` serve the handshake alone. Reassembly and concurrent messages carry bounds; part size follows the Noise limit after header and sealing overhead. Missing, duplicate, mixed-kind, overlong and post-close parts refuse. The reason: a large sync frame needs bounded, unambiguous carriage. The ruling flips on a smaller measured layout that passes the same large-sync, replay, memory-bound and cross-kind vectors.
* ''Sharing after the gate'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). The proven same-operator class establishes transport alone. The session's proven face and scoped document view decide each document. A place arm reaches infrastructure alone; a PersonaGroup grant admits a sibling vessel of the same face and grants no blanket document access. Two tabs on one laptop wearing different faces see different books. The reason: transport kinship grants no document readership. The ruling flips on a scoped-view proof that another key carries the same face with exactly the same facets.
* ''A device set aside'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). Each vessel face-key certificate binds its face key to its certified device edge. Before sorting a presentation as same-operator, the gate consults the fleet edge's shadow for that device edge and refuses while the mapping or fold reads unreadable. The wire carries only the face-scoped key. A restart and two-face witness gate the cutover. The reason: removal of a device must cut every face it wore. The ruling flips on causal withdrawal of every affected face certificate proven to reach every gate with no gap.
* ''The contract seam'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). N2, N4 and N5 stand handed back. Train W owns the admit-carriage hunk of `nexus-contract.ts` for the cutover, keeps N's anchor and prior-roll checks, and lands the contract, `device-admit.ts`, fleet answer, verifier and vectors as one signed-shape change. The reason: one signature shape must cross every producer and reader together. The ruling flips on a proven independent carriage boundary that preserves those checks and signed bytes.
* ''The founder's certificate'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). A founder self-issues its vessel face-key certificate through the checked issue door and withdrawal history a joiner uses. The reason: the founder's session needs the same chain and removal law. The ruling flips on a verified equivalent issue path with the same history.
* ''Routes at the cutover'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). Walker arms, the sibling dial and the herm membership relay ship SHUT by name; H opens walkers with its live grant reader, W9 opens the sibling at face scope, and an explicit Nexus opens the herm relay. The oracle read face and reader convert where browser boot reads the oracle in production; elsewhere they ship SHUT by name after measuring `pullAndVerifyOracle`'s caller. The waystone reads the Nexus's one public answer before it trusts its engine. An untested old-wire fallback counts as open. The reason: a closed route grants no silent legacy authority. The ruling flips on a proven gate-native caller for each shut route.
* ''The floor announcement'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). `waking-floor-announce` signs one canonical body per carried Nexus under that Nexus's floor bundle signing key: version, Nexus AID, floor route pin digest, floor standing and causal position. It travels only inside an authenticated socket or relay relation. The reader checks the already pinned floor pin, domain, Nexus, route and causal order, and treats the body as a peer hint alone. The reason: a floor hint must neither bootstrap its own trust nor replay across Nexuses. The ruling flips on a smaller body that still refuses cross-Nexus replay, stale replacement and first-contact self-certification.
* ''Fleet join serves every face'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). `fleet join` names its face with `--face <index>`, default `0`; a nonzero face carries that face's certificates, grants and address secret. The reason: a person wears several faces, and each face needs its own fleet relation. The ruling flips on a face-independent proof that carries the same per-face authority without mixing identities.
* ''A realm's steward map holds leaves'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-16). The co-signed `keptBy` names fill `realmStewardMap`: each name a steward's worn per-Nexus face or a Handle, with the bags it keeps. That map answers which bags a steward keeps, never a root question; the realm consult's ROOT map stays `peerContractNymMap`, filled only by a root edge proven at the wire (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/two-maps`>>). The reason: a leaf that answers a root question links the two maps the doctrine keeps apart. The ruling flips if a root edge proven at the reading seat comes to fill a realm map, which then stands beside this one under the root map's own name.
* ''The re-cut's eight forks'' (house, under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-31). The cut reads left eight forks; the house talked each out two rounds against canon and a Gaia and an Elyncia story, and none survived to the operator: `daemonAuth` lands as one pure shared builder in mesh; node and browser pin one boot list; seven boot cells refuse a read before set by name; the share verdict lands one law per relation, measured before it merges; the stage-cut and the verb math take pure deciders and a pure module first; the shared stages converge on node's fuller form; the login suffix retires and custody resolves its home once at open; and a removed joiner never re-enters on an invite minted before the removal, its mechanism decided by the red. The floor stands at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/test-recut-road`>> #/the-floor, and the T rows carry each settlement.

* ''The channel of intent'' (operator, 2026-10-20: //"Approve your leans, land the full models into the architecture now"//). The daemon wiki keeps working memory; a face's intents travel on that face's own intent plane, `@intents-<tag>`, a fifth face plane beside the four; a vessel's own records rest in its daemon bag, unmirrored; the face's pins rest in the face's custody and `@persona`. The approved leans: the plane stands from the first landing, never staged in the daemon bag; the winner reads by the intent's own preference list, else the lowest `H(atom ‖ intent id)`, and never elects; a single-effect fleet-wide verb never addresses "any device", and a human answer may name its executor; a claim never expires, a device's staleness stays a local suspicion, and the operator's release always wins; an `every` intent reaches a later-joined device unless its bound names a roster edge; a far agent reaches the plane through a relay seat, opt-in per face and ring-gated, its research before its build; an intent voids by supersession, a revoked delegation, a passed roster edge or the operator's cancel; compaction folds by the operator's act, never a timer; a local verb that touches its own vessel alone bypasses the plane and leaves its receipt in that vessel's bag. The plane carries a hand's decisions that cross devices, never its motion (the rubric, <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-intent-plane`>> #/the-rubric). Row `I1`.
* ''The place signer's fifth duty'' (operator, 2026-10-20, approving the controller's lean). A place's finish at another place's gate, `GATE_PLACE_FINISH_DOMAIN`, joins the place signer's floor duties: a faceless place that reads another's floor performs a floor act. Each of the five duties binds its domain by an exact head at byte zero, in one table beside the allowlist; `X0` moves the framing into the signer.

* ''Sync carries; verification decides'' (operator, 2026-10-20, approving the research at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/identity-invariants-2026`>> and the controller's leans). A mutable CRDT carries identity claims and never decides them. Each face holds, on each device, a ''sealed identity anchor'' in its own custody (a D row): its PersonaGroup, its persona-KEL prefix, a commitment to its root, its device edge's digest, and a known frontier that never falls. Only founding, admission and the recovery rites (recovery contact rotation, bundle restore, root cards) write an anchor; sync never writes one. Founding and admission acts, device edges and lease advances travel as signed records in the face's `@persona` plane, a signed-entry log every reader verifies against the anchor and the KEL; two signed acts at one sequence surface as a fork, never a CRDT winner. The face planes hold verified projections. A device reads freely; an act that widens authority (admit, grant, re-key, seal to a reader) checks the sealed frontier and refuses a target that names a newer bound, its receipt naming the frontier it acted under. Transparency and witnesses for equivocation wait for their own row. Wave C carries the build inside `K15` step 6.
* ''Wave B's forks'' (operator, 2026-10-20, approving the controller's leans). A fresh PersonaGroup mints a fresh atom, never an orphan's seed. A tab reads its face's pins from its custody anchor before it opens its session, so the tab's own Repo retires; and each face holds its own social planes in the browser, keyed by PersonaGroup as on the node. Both ride Wave C with the anchor.

* ''The order of the next waves'' (operator, 2026-10-20, approving the controller's leans). Wave C (`K15` step 6 under the identity anchor) runs next, with `W26`'s slices 3, 4 and 6 beside it on the floor's own ground, their composer hunks passing to the controller as patches, and slice 5 after it, since its accepted floor rests as a face's sealed row (a D row under the face's own FCK once Wave C lands); `I1` follows the anchor; the far agent's relay seat takes its own research after `I1`; `G37`'s renames ride `X0`'s bake; the sensorium resumes after `X0`, on the operator's word.

* ''The edge frontier'' (operator, 2026-10-20, approving the controller's leans after the research at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/edge-frontier-2026`>>). Nothing signed today lets a reader tell a device never set aside from a raise erased or withheld, so the house adds a signed ''edge frontier'' per board, its own domain-separated certificate type, the persona KEL resolving its signer's key. Every hand that can raise on a board signs its own stream's frontier (a fleet board's root; a realm board's charter quorum, with its holder where vouch edges raise), and a frontier commits its counted acts through its boundary as a content-addressed snapshot, its parent frontier, and its roster and epoch lineage. A raise shadows its target at once for every reader that sees it, and counts toward widening once its signer registers it into the next frontier. Sync always carries acts, frontiers and snapshots. A widening act (admit, grant, a content key sealed or re-sealed to a reader) reads its target against a frontier of every raising hand that extends any head the reader pinned and includes every raise it has seen; it acts as of that frontier, its receipt naming it, and waits as unknown where none qualifies. Every fleet and realm founds with a signed empty genesis frontier, and an admit bundle carries the current frontier and its snapshot. The fleet board's frontier stands built with Wave C, and the realm boards' leg landed with K17-R in PR #97. Row `K17`.

!!! The fresh start
* ''Old shapes burn'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-28: //"O-takiage the old shapes & shape gates now. Its design time crud buildup that unfortunately causes agents to repeat these patterns. Early alpha law holds - we dont know the final shapes yet ... after founding these guards become important, so we should have clean design space to think through"//). Source never recognises bytes or shapes from before a format cut, to refuse them by name or to read them tolerantly: a cut re-founds. Live refusals of today's shapes (a wrong key, a torn row, a foreign face) stand. The evergreen witness holds an `old-shape` class at zero; boundary guards for pinned bytes get designed after the founding, in clean space (porch bundle six, PR #109).
* ''No pave or wipe command'' — such a command carries too much risk. See #/fresh-start.


!!! The test refactor
* ''The test architecture re-cuts before the founding'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31, lifting the 2026-10-29 ruling that set it after): train T runs beside the founding road and never stalls it (#/stride-t-recut). Until each layer re-cuts, lanes land large batches and run the heavy battery once per batch.

