<<~ ahu slot=`#/decisions`>>

!! Decisions ~ what shapes unbuilt work, and why

The hosted walk's own mechanics (the token, the grant, the spent-set, the hosting doc) read in
`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-invite`. The decisions below constrain a slice
still on the road.

* ''One sorter, inside the gate, before the verdict.'' Every vessel socket opens through the sealed face gate's attach, verdict and open sequence; the gate sorts before the verdict, so posture reads once at admission and never at share time. `GateSessionChannel` opens only after the verdict (`face-gate.ts`, `gate-session-channel.ts`). One gate stands per (Nexus, route).
* ''The knock.'' A gate answers an upgrade only on a path derived from its own key (`gateKnock` via `knockSegment` in `packages/lararium-mesh/src/gate-bundle.ts`), so a dialer holding no pin draws no HTTP 101 — the WireGuard mac1 shape. The path still rides the upgrade in the clear on plain `ws`; full client-proves-first waits for Noise IK (R-WIRE).
* ''Silence, one deadline.'' No denial frame and no close codes: one drop deadline drawn at accept, so verify time leaks nothing and a PRIVATE stranger looks exactly like a failed proof.
* ''The multi-Nexus fold.'' A gate answers strangers if ANY Nexus it stands in reads OPEN. One gate key serves every Nexus a vessel carries, so this rule reduces exactly to per-Nexus posture once each Nexus carries its own wire key (R-WIRE) — not an interim.
* ''Carriage keys on the guest's PROVEN leaf.'' A hearth-issued stub would read as a bearer capability to another guest's record and as a hearth-minted cross-lineage linker. The leaf already proves on every socket, so keying on it adds no linkage the hearth lacks; a fresh leaf opens a fresh carriage (#/open, carriage across leaves).
* ''One store door.'' A command that touches the store runs inside the standing vessel over the owner-only socket, else owns the store itself for that moment. A second Repo beside a live holder writes bytes the replica never sees. `tools/direct-repo-witness.sh` names the two lawful `new Repo(` sites and reads any other as DRIFT.
* ''No hearth-local deny set.'' Kapae and the roll suffice; a deny set would make a seizable list.
* ''No node carriage.'' A node serves as its own durable store and no story walks its carriage; ruling N holds as a sorter property and W as the hearth's service. Node carry would open a new user story, never owed by N or W.
* ''Hosting stays on the worn persona leaf.'' ROOT-ON-FOUNDER keeps a persona root on one vessel, so one face cannot host from two stores unseen; the first-roll rail surfaces the remaining case. The face-switch act inherits it.
* ''A cliff takes a funnel, then one assembled commit.'' Where one shape change crosses three packages, the fixtures funnel through one helper first; narrow builders each write one patch against a fixed contract; one lander proves them together in a scratch worktree and commits once, so the main tree never holds half a flip. Three cliffs stand on the road: the charter flip, the Nexus rename and the socket cutover.
* ''The socket's handshake changes once.'' R-WIRE and the face proof build as ONE wire train: the signed admit, then the fleet-join door, then one socket cutover (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof`>>). The tree between those steps reads no earlier shape; it stands as a build order, never a compatibility mode.
* ''A pure library names its caller.'' A module built ahead of its consumer stays out of the package barrel until the commit that consumes it. Until a production path calls it, a module stands specified in code, never built.
* ''`vessel found` keeps a charterless road.'' The Stable founding act becomes the HEARTH's rite; a herm still founds as a place with no face, and a joiner still founds through an admit.
* ''The carriage verifier holds the root'': it checks the epoch, the signature AND a subject root it holds locally, and refuses by name without that root.
* ''Recovery contact placement'' reads as advice the house names at enrolment, never a refusal. ''QR pairing'' shows a check string on both screens after the channel opens.
* ''The floor vessel key drops its next-key commitment'', inside the one identity train. It changes the vessel's signed inception bytes; the operator may overturn it.
* ''A long-silent fleet device'' catches up before it acts: a presentation carries its presenter's frontier of the face's withdrawal history, and a gate that holds a later one answers `unconfirmed` and hands over what the device lacks. No clock; a house reading the operator may overturn (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-withdrawal-proof`>>).
* ''The strangers' dial drops the NEWEST arrival'' when its slots fill; a slot frees by the operator's answer or a roll.
* ''Every browser leaf counts as evictable'': `storage.persist()` refuses in a plain tab, so no leaf holds the only copy of anything, and no passkey slot stands as the only way in (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/device-capabilities-2026`>>).
* ''A restarted always-on node serves no hosted phone until a human unlocks.'' The resolver and the terminator keep answering, so the page loads and the sync waits; the page says so in one line, until the ember lands.
* ''A crossing gets recorded, never interior life.'' Only an authority, vessel, federation or externally meaningful crossing becomes distributed canon (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/mandate-activity-provenance`>>).
* ''A package names no vessel.'' Every entity starts at the herm floor; a seated persona lights the Lararium stack over it. Only a lararium lights a Pronaos, and a herm mirrors a house's published bytes by CID (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/pronaos#/the-placement`>>).
* ''Deferred by the house'': realm feeding (two carriers disagree on max over per-writer slots against a blank merge; it waits for the first-realm arc), community exit, and funding, where payment may buy capacity and never standing.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-road`>>

!! The road ~ what stays unbuilt, in the order a builder walks it

''The organizing law'': identity bytes (the Nexus AID, the device edge, the dyad scope, the seats, the
sentinel) change ONCE, inside one train. ''The owner law'': each slice lands its logic in modules, and one
named owner per step commits the one compose hunk in `open-node-vessel.ts` and `open-browser-vessel.ts`;
every other lane stops at a module boundary. Each lane's first commit carries its canon. ''The fence''
(#/rulings) bounds the whole road: every slice that changes a shape lands before the second operator seats a key in the first Nexus.

''The order'': HEARTH → GROUND → SUBSTRATE → HARBOR → KAHU PLANE → ONE FACE → TWO HEARTHS, with train T's re-cut beside every path (#/the-order ranks the slots). The re-found's train
rides inside it — R-ENGINE's fold and R-CUSTODY's seal in SUBSTRATE; R-CHARTER → R-WIRE, then R-KAHU,
R-ENGINE's offers and R-EMBER, in the KAHU PLANE. Where the train's older order (the engine after the wire)
and the six paths disagree, operator-ruled order governs: the engine fold and the custody
seal land early, and the container's tail lands before the charter train.

!!! 0 · HEARTH ~ the memory-stage hearth (no identity bytes; leads the road; rows in #/stride-0-hearth)
* ''The walk'': one machine, one operator. A node founds and stands at its floor, unlocks by the operator's hand, and serves its own page from its own origin; the page stands a browser vessel at its floor, binds a passphrase slot before any face secret, and joins the node's face through the fleet-join acts; a page edit reaches `<LAR_ROOT>/wikis/daemon/`, a CLI write shows in the page, and the MCP seat reads and writes the same wiki (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/pronaos`>> #/the-first-arrival; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract`>> #/the-hands). The harness walks it on a scratch root through the test door; the operator walks it on the hearth that holds the house's memory.
* ''The stage'': tags and one index view on the daemon wiki, never a new store or plane. A working stage holds what the controller's scratch files hold today; an episodic tier keeps scout reports, walks and incidents; survivors reach `bags/` carriers by promotion under a person's ruling, and the fire of otakiage forgets what landed (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/otakiage`>>). No row reads a clock: a row's freshness reads as the commit or wave that last re-checked it, and a recurrence counts repeat incidents.
* ''The seat'': an agent writes the stage as a seat, never as a key; its writes land in the working layer, and the profile gate refuses it every authority-changing verb (`F11`).
* ''The owner law holds here too'': a hearth row reaches `open-node-vessel.ts` and `open-browser-vessel.ts` only through the named owner, and the shore's files (`packages/lararium-web/**`) take the hearth's page rows as a shore lane.

!!! 1 · GROUND ~ the residue (no identity bytes; waits on nothing; rides beside SUBSTRATE on disjoint files)
* ''The receipt ledger's direct leg'': the three receipts of #/rulings stand where a CLI meets its vessel (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/receipt-ledger`>>). The leg across a herm rides THE WIRE (`W12` of #/stride-1-spine).
* ''`KeyhiveIdentitySlot` production wiring.''
* ''The evergreen sweep and custody's edges'', itemized as `G6` (#/after-the-seat) and `G7` (#/stride-1-spine).
* ''Measures'': the full-corpus per-verb flush re-measure; pack-PIN recursion; a production `assertBodyIndexTier` caller.
* ''Canon direction the build owes'': canon crosses hearth → leaf; a leaf never writes the hearth's canon documents, and edits land in the working layer. The admitted leaf-side mount flag stops mount-path writes, while Repo sync carries those documents in both directions.
* ''A binding lands after its document rests'': a slot mint records the binding only on the vessel store's acknowledgment (the rest door, `doc:rest` / `doc:rested`); a mint no binding names stays listed (`lares vessel unbound`), and nothing removes one.

!!! 2 · SUBSTRATE ~ two SOLO relays, the fold first, then the seal
Each runs one spirit per commit, and every commit leaves HEAD building. No other node lane runs beside the
seal: it owns the compose hunk.

''R-ENGINE, the fold.''
* The R-ENGINE fold target dependency line, in fixed order: automerge-repo and its four listed adapters at alpha.5 → `@automerge/automerge` 3.4.1 → `@keyhive/keyhive` 0.3.0-alpha.1; no Subduction or Onomancy dependency → the genesis re-bake (`GENESIS_ENGINE_CID`, every fixture holding the hearth true-name, the untracked CAS in the same commit) → the fold's own live proof on the rebuilt image, with a pushed shard run reading green. The manifests hold all three dependency steps (`packages/lararium-node/package.json`, `packages/lararium-keyhive/package.json`); a Keyhive Archive and a cap event carry no format tag, so bytes one Keyhive version wrote refuse under another and a version move re-founds every vessel. The fold's own live proof on the rebuilt image stands owed.
* TW5 core and the memetic-wikitext bootstrap fold into `engineCid`; the vocabulary leaves the engine as content; the coal-only seed carries no `schemaVersion`; the island `schema_version` drops its counter.

''R-CUSTODY, the seal'' (P3-SEAL).
* ''What stands sealed'': the custody root and the `identity-anchors` family: the keyhive archive, each face's own veil archive (`veil-archive-g<group digest>.bin` on the node, keyed by its PersonaGroup; the browser's `veil-archive-h<N>` row still keys by handle index until `K15` step 6), each face's anchors and the anchor roster. A founding seals them from its first write and hands out every keyhive Archive it stood before the provider leaves; the veil derives from the sealed custody root; a seat (`custody-seat.ts`) carries one holder per identity home, and each row reads whole or refuses by name, never empty. A creator that stands again restores from its Archive: cap events carry membership and never a creator's key agreement state. A stand mints one contact card and its Archive leaves after that mint, so the Archive lineage opens every prekey the identity ever presented. A browser vessel keeps the Archives its founding and its admit hand out in its keystore beside the device seed, in the clear, until browser custody wraps both (`browser-keyhive-archive.ts`).
* ''Every writer family rests under its custody key'': the carrier table (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table`>>; `vault-carriers.ts`) names `floor` and `floor-plain` outside the vessel's keys, `hot` and `cold` under the vessel key, `serving` under SK, and `outbox` under an outbox key the vessel key wraps. SK mints at the founding; a human unlock opens it through the vessel key's entry in its serving tree (`sk-slots`, a bare `floor` row; `serving-key.ts`, `custody-session.ts`). Serving rows and ember slots stay owed (`E2`); sealed outbox parcels stand, while ship and delete stay owed (`K13`). Each sealed row passes through the one sealed writer (sidecar → re-open → compare → rename, never sealing in place) and the sealed open. The per-face backup bundle stands built, cut and restore.
* ''No keyring stands'' (operator ruling, #/rulings): the code holds no per-Nexus convergence secret. No custody row, seat mint, admission delivery or charter commitment carries one, and a persona admission's signed transcript names no keyring digest. A private body seals under its bag's content key, which reaches its readers through a lockbox (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/keys`>>). A seat's act and a roll mint nothing beside the charter epoch. The same ruling's standing law binds every later slice: let a counter, wherever one appears, order one writer's own emissions only; keep the face at the session capability; keep every verified fork head visible; retain by causal evidence.
* ''The handle-book'': the recogniser's handle-book rests sealed under the S key. Nothing at its path reads as a book that recognises nobody; a row that stands and does not open, or opens to another shape, refuses by name at every read and every write, and a save lands only over nothing or over a snapshot that reads whole (`packages/lararium-node/src/node-circle-store.ts`). The follow graph holds one home, the persona-scoped circles plane the daemon's circle verbs write, and no custody row carries an edge.
* ''The fleet-join family'': one face's root-signed answer carries its device edge, signed gate carriage, island reach, sealed address secret, vessel face-key certificates and add-a-face grants. A join lands through one checked door. `device-admit/v1` names the internal signed edge format, and `cabal-join` still names people and realm admission.
* ''The vessel-identity family'': the persona roster, the pet-name map, the declaration map and the published-face record rest sealed under the S key as hot rows, and the worn face lives as session state and rests in no row (`F9`); each persona root rests as a cold row, opened for one act; the floor vessel key rests beside its signed inception, every load proves that inception, and no next-key seed or commitment rides it. The roster commits a root: a mint writes the root row, then the roster line that carries the root's public key, so a reading of a public key answers off the hot roster and opens no root. A root row with no roster line reads as an orphan: no reader holds it, `lares persona list` names it, and the same mint run again adopts it. A roster line whose root row went missing, or names another key, refuses by name at the act that needs the seed, and nothing mints over it. A hot row with nothing at its path reads as holding nothing; a row that stands and does not open, or opens to another shape, refuses by name at every read and every write. A vessel key mints only inside a held custody, and lands with its inception under the custody's one claim: the key file commits the mint, so two acts never commit two keys, and a boot loads a key and never mints one. The floor reads no row of the family, so a carried-Nexus reading by persona (a seated chair, a kept consent) answers empty until the key arrives, and a sovereign composition reads the roster whole before it stands (`packages/lararium-node/src/node-vessel-identity.ts`). A floor composition reads no roster, so it shuts every fleet lane: it sorts every device as set aside, sets a proven sibling aside, and its fetch door reads no peer as fleet, the pinned join gate's key included (`packages/lararium-node/src/floor-fleet-lanes.ts`). It still folds the one fleet board its own device edge names in the clear, so the Binding Gate reads this vessel's own seat at every standing.
* ''The device recovery share'': each face's device share rests as one COLD row under the S key, opened for the act that mints it and the quorum that assembles it (`packages/lararium-node/src/recovery-share-store.ts`). Nothing at a face's path reads as none landed; a row that stands and does not open, or opens to another shape, refuses the read and the write by name, so a wrong key replaces no share and no split lands over one it could not read. A share lands once per generation: a second split of the same generation refuses, since it would orphan the off-device shares a person already holds. A split hands the person both off-device shares before the device share rests, so a run that stops between the two leaves nothing sealed and the same command splits afresh.
* ''The walk records'': each hearth a vessel walks at keeps one HOT row sealed under the S key, named by a digest of that hearth's gate key (`packages/lararium-node/src/node-walk-store.ts`). A record holds bearer invites, the hearth's grant and one receipt per carried document (`{ cid: "blake3:<hex>", ref, key }`, whose `key` opens that document), so it counts as a secret row. The row rests in the storage home of the custody seat: the store takes no directory, so no caller files it beside a document store the vessel key never spans. Nothing at a hearth's path reads as no walk there; a row that stands and does not open, opens to another shape, or rests filed under another hearth's name refuses the read, the list and the update by name, and an update seals a whole record or nothing. Each change reads, decides and seals under one custody holder, so one invite leaves a wallet once. A floor composition reads no walk record and presents no walk; a fresh browser device stands at the floor under its `#floor=` card, and a joined face crosses under its root-signed pin. A key that locks under a live walk costs the walk one message, which it logs. `lares walk`, `walk take` and `walk invite` ride the custody door (`walk.state` · `walk.take` · `walk.invite`): a carried invite as a verb's argument, or an invite as a verb's outcome, would rest in the daemon's document in the clear.
* ''The hosting rows'': a hearth's hosting state, each spend and its walkers' carriage rest as HOT rows sealed under the S key, in the storage home of the custody seat (`packages/lararium-node/src/hosting-store.ts`, `hosting-carry.ts`). The sealed writer writes a whole file and appends to none, so each spend lands as ONE row of its own, named by a digest of its nonce or marker: the row's presence IS the burn, and it reads, decides and seals under one custody holder. The spend row commits a redemption, and what rides ahead of it re-derives: the grant from the epoch, the leaf, the nonce and the claim, so a crash before the answer replays as `retry`; the operator's counts from the rows standing, by name. A roll's state row commits the roll, and the hosting doc re-derives from it: the next roll and each sovereign stand land an act the sealed state holds and the doc lacks (`landHostingActs`). The sweep of dead epochs' rows passes through the seat's removal door (`removeCustodyRow`), which removes only a row this vessel's key opens; a spend that sealed after a roll retired its epoch burns nothing live and leaves with the next sweep. Each carried value rests as one row whose name carries its address and its length, so a guest's bytes read off its rows and no counter stands beside them. One process serves a hearth's carriage (the standing vessel's own gate, under its store claim), and its contacts and deposits run in turn, so a quota reads the bytes the last deposit left and a reclaim never discards on a reading a contact moved meanwhile. A row that stands and does not open refuses by name: a burn nobody can read never reads as unspent, and a carried value nobody can open never reads as absent. A floor composition reads no hosting row, so a locked hearth sorts no socket as a walker. `lares host`, `host roll` and `host invite` ride the custody door (`host.state` · `host.roll` · `host.invite`): a minted invite as a verb's outcome would rest in the daemon's document in the clear.
* ''The seal ceremony'': the growth rite's two rows rest HOT under the S key in the seal home (`packages/lararium-node/src/seal-ceremony.ts`): `transition-pending.json` prepares a crossing, and `transitions.json` commits its record. A seal verifies the crossing whole, appends its record (the commit point), then removes the pending row, only while that row still holds the crossing it sealed; a seal that committed and stopped resumes to one kept record and no pending row, and kept records with no pending row read clean. A chair's own seat key signs for the ceremony behind the custody door (`seat-key-store.ts`), and only what the standing state licenses: `seal.sign` names a seat by its public key and signs a charter roll that steps from the standing head, reveals at least that head's count of the seats it committed one digest each, seats only keys this vessel holds and re-arms every seat it seats, where the charter's lineage verifies and its head binds the seated roster; it refuses every other image however well formed, and a persona root holds no seat row, so it signs no roll; the rite's hands mark through `crossing.open` · `bind` · `sign` · `witness` · `seal`, which take no image and sign the crossing that stands open (a face's seat keys sign; a witness's root attests). The rite arms itself behind the same door (`seat.arm-genesis`, `seat.arm-roll`): each standing chair's seat key mints there, each chair's NEXT key prints once as a cold-key card the operator reads back before the charter moves (a staged test root alone stands in), a roll adopts each card's key as its chair's seat key, and a superseded seat key retires once no held charter seats it and no open crossing names it (`seat.retire`). An earlier charter shape reads by name (`nexusCharterShape`) and licenses nothing. ''No command file writes a custody row'': a census derives every sealed row's name, every write door and every writer function from the carrier table and the custody modules, and reads red on a command source that calls a door, names a writer function, or names a row beside a file-system write of its own (`packages/lares-cli/tests/custody-write-census.test.ts`).
* ''The face proof's rows'': `face-proof-key` rests COLD, one face's proof key at one Nexus, held by the root-holding vessel alone, its file named for the face so that face's backup bundle carries it; `vessel-face-key` rests HOT, this vessel's own face-scoped key, and stays on its vessel, outside every bundle. Fleet join calls the certificate and grant path; the removal and gate session paths still need the socket cutover. Two doors on the custody seat sign and return (`signAsFace`, `signAsVesselFace`): each signs exactly the image handed in under the key its scope names and hands out no secret, and a store never lands over another standing key (`packages/lararium-node/src/face-key-custody.ts`; the library names the shapes in `FACE_PROOF_CUSTODY_ROWS`).
* ''The gate's two root doors'' (specified in code; no composer calls them until the socket cutover): a floor gate's bundle derives through the floor key's door, which answers at a locked vessel, hands back a fresh child and never the floor key (`vesselFloorKeyDoor`, `packages/lararium-node/src/node-vessel-identity.ts`); a sovereign gate's bundle derives through the custody seat's door, which answers only while the seat holds its key (`custodyGateDoor`, `packages/lararium-node/src/custody-seat.ts`). The doors carry two types, so no line swaps them by accident; a root refuses the other tier's door by name; each seat's door refuses the other tier's child; and a weld holds the two mints to the two seats (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange`>>).
* ''The gate's memory rows'' (specified in code; no gate calls them until the socket cutover): a sovereign gate's withdrawal memory rests one row per face key in the vessel's storage home, named by the face's proof-subject digest and sealed under the S key, so a locked vessel's disk names no face key (`gate-memory` in the carrier table; `packages/lararium-node/src/gate-memory-rows.ts`). Each write reads, decides and seals under one custody holder, and refuses where it would drop an event the row holds; a locked seat refuses by name. An older copy of a row restored by hand makes a gate forget until the next honest vessel dials, and no fence stands against that hand. A row that stands and does not fold whole refuses by name and reports it (`GateMemoryRowTorn`): the asking gate falls silent, no keep writes over the row, and a person repairs it by hand, where a row read as nothing would let a fresh memory admit a vessel the row once denied. No backup bundle carries the row: a vessel that stands again knows nothing (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-withdrawal-proof`>>).
* ''The session and the boot'': the daemon stands at the floor holding no S key and unlocks on a custody frame of its owner-only socket (`unlock` · `lock` · `custody` · `act` · `backup`, `packages/lararium-node/src/custody-frames.ts`), handled ahead of every verb, so the credential the CLI reads on its TTY reaches no receipt, outcome or log. One holder keeps the opened S key in node-main (`custody-holder.ts`); an unlock tears the standing composition down and stands a fresh one on the same floor (`open-node-vessel.ts`), a lock returns it to the floor, and three failed unlocks spend a boot's attempts. A composition that refuses above the floor leaves the floor standing and names its fault once. The settle sweeps a sidecar whose write never landed. The test door (`tools/unlock-test-custody.mjs`) opens marked scratch roots alone, and the marker earns one loud line in `vault status` and the boot log. Every boot composes at the floor: nothing stored lifts it. A daemon island holds no S key, so its archive exports cross the custody write door (`custody:write`) and the main thread seals them. Every broad sync port, the daemon island's and each wiki island's, crosses one hardened facade on both ends: an arrival that reads as no sync message drops at the port and never reaches a listener that would throw on it (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/island-repo.ts`). The daemon island's gates ask the main thread what a device's edge reads on its fleet's board through the dyad door (`dyad:ask`), which answers `aside` wherever it cannot read. Each fold of the fleet's edge board sets off the key side's settle on an unlocked vessel, with no timer and no person: the main thread places the island's `seat-duties` verb, awaits its answer and keeps the signed rolls (`packages/lararium-node/src/seat-settle.ts`; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/what-the-code-holds`>>). Those two doors and the rest door answer the daemon island's channel alone: each worker's class binds at its spawn (`packages/lararium-node/src/worker-handle.ts`), a wiki island reaches the fetch door alone while its custody write and its rest ask refuse by name and its dyad ask draws `aside`, and a session island reaches no vessel door. A command that acts with no vessel standing opens its own key session at the first sealed row it touches (`packages/lares-cli/src/offline-custody.ts`): a terminal prompt, and under a marked scratch root the published test route. A sealed row's act rides an `act` frame of the custody door and never a store verb: one typed table bounds the acts (`packages/lararium-node/src/sealed-acts.ts`), a standing vessel answers with no second prompt, and beside a standing vessel a command opens no key session of its own, while a root's recovery split refuses there (`packages/lares-cli/src/sealed-act-door.ts`). The fleet mirror of a pet-name and a declared Handle rides that door too: `selves.carry` names a face and which name, the vessel's main thread reads the name off its sealed row and writes it onto that face's own persona plane and no other face's, only where that plane's rest seals (a clear rest refuses the carry and the name stays on its row), `lares persona sync` fills and never replaces, and no verb takes or answers a face's own name (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape`>>).
* ''The per-face backup bundle, its cut'': `lares vault backup --face <N> --out <file>` cuts one face's bundle through the standing vessel's custody door, under the law at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-cut`>>. The carrier table's bundle column picks the rows, the frame asks the vessel's own route again, the daemon island seals the memory section to the pair the bundle key derives, and the command binds the bundle's routes in its own process (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/face-bundle.ts`; `packages/lararium-node/src/face-bundle-cut.ts`, `custody-frames.ts`; `packages/lares-cli/src/vault-backup.ts`). The handle-book tags each record with the face that recognised it, so a bundle carries its own face's records alone. A node vessel's daemon island stands its content-key holder off the document it owns; no content key mints on a production vessel until a session signer stands, so a production cut carries an empty memory section.
* ''The per-face backup bundle, its restore'': `lares vault restore <file>` brings a face back onto a fresh vessel as a recovery act, under the law at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore`>>. The vessel checks the root against the newest key history it reads, seals one commit row, lands the carried rows additively, and lights the face through the face rite: a new seat under the same fixed prefix (`packages/lararium-node/src/face-bundle-restore.ts`; the act table's `bundle.restore` and `bundle.import` rows; `packages/lares-cli/src/vault-restore.ts`). The rehearsal runs end to end on scratch roots, on the real doors and on standing vessels of the built tree. `--memory` lands the bundle's content keys through the island's import door; on a production vessel it refuses `signer-unavailable` until the wire cutover opens a session signer.
* ''The custody door and its census'': `lares vault` answers seven verbs (`status` · `unlock` · `lock` · `bind` · `unbind` · `backup` · `restore`), and a credential rides a terminal alone: no flag, environment variable or pipe carries one. `vault status` gives ONE reading of each standing file, derived from the carrier table: a `hot` or `cold` row reads sealed or reads as a fault, a row outside the S key prints the law it rests bare under or reads as a fault where the file breaks that law, and a private file in the three homes that no row names prints by name (`vault-carriers.ts` `outsideTheTable`; `custody-session.ts` `custodyStatus`). `vault unbind --rotate` runs as one act: the rotation's census and its opening of every carrier pass before any byte moves, so a carrier that refuses the rotation leaves the slot bound. `tools/custody-census-witness.sh` founds a vessel on a marked scratch root with the built tree and refuses an owner-only file, a seal or a sidecar outside the table; it carries that rotation to its end on the founded vessel. The law, each bare row's reason and the residue a row's name leaves at rest read at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table`>>.

!!! 3 · HARBOR ~ the container's tail
* ''A persona door before `up`'': `tools/lares-container.sh` routes CLI acts through the offline door before `up`; after a successful act in a standing vessel the host shim reads the standing verdict and restarts the vessel on `restand`. The verdict reads a LIT face alone, so a `persona wear` that changes the worn face on a standing vessel waits on ONE FACE.
* ''LAN candidate discovery'': the module and wiring stand off by default; `LAR_LAN_DISCOVERY=announce` enables announcements without keys. A real-LAN run and the network mode remain owed (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/lan-discovery`).
* ''Household HTTPS'': the compose `https` profile supplies a terminator and a resolver slot, the `issuer` profile supplies a one-shot DNS-01 issuer that reads the registrar's credential from two owner-read files outside both mounts (`docker-compose.surface.yml`; `tools/household-https-witness.sh` walks issuance against a test authority), the resolver answers each household name with its own address from one records file the operator keeps (`tools/household-resolver-witness.sh` walks it against a test zone), and `deviceAdmitSyncUrl` derives relay reach from `LAR_PUBLIC_URL`. The node entry names itself by that setting alone: a boot without one, or with one that names loopback, says so in one loud line, and the first request that arrives under another host logs once per boot (`packages/lararium-node/src/reach-face-watch.ts`). The operator supplies the domain and the credential files. Owed: the renewal verb with its reload, and a first live issuance. No device takes an install (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/household-https`, `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/secure-context`).
* ''The harnesses stand on the image'': harnesses run the built image by digest and bind no source tree; the mesh scenarios name their own compose project (`MESH_PROJECT`) and feed their browser vessels from the image; the browser CI runner alone reads the repository read-only. Each harness opens the vessel it staged through the test custody door and reads a hearth as stood only once that door answered an open key; `mesh-scenarios all`, `harbor-walk-witness`, the crossing and herm witnesses, the browser walks and the household HTTPS witness all stand on that shape (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/the-harnesses`).
* ''The release manifest'': `tools/release-manifest.mjs` emits the record shape; a real multi-arch index, its push and signing remain owed.

!!! 4 · KAHU PLANE ~ the realms, the signer, and the one AID train
Build order: the realm and signer shapes against the rulings; then R-CHARTER, the one train that moves
identity bytes; then R-WIRE, so every pin changes once.

* ''Realms'': a vessel stands one plane per realm, and a verb names its realm (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/many-realms`>>). A cabal reads as its relation closure and takes no key or roster of its own. A cabal realm's documents cross between two operators once the realm's member consult answers for a peer.
* ''The steward signer'': the worn face or a quorum Handle, never the root (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-steward-signer`>>). The seal, the fold and the steward door stand in code; the composing vessel's `realm-bag` verb takes the door through the compose owner's hand. The docker `meme` scenario then proves it by hand at "B edits, promotes", in the asker's order: B asks to keep the bag with her worn face, and A consents.
* ''Thresholded admission and promotion'' stand built: the chartered classes derive from the charter's own seats and threshold (`chartered-classes.ts`); promotion approves the exact bytes' hash with purpose, surface, class and epoch (`promotion-approval.ts`, `lares nexus promote open|sign|check`); Kahu-cabal admission stands as its own act at the high-impact threshold, where a vouch admits nobody (`kahu-cabal-admission.ts`, `lares nexus cabal`) (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/chartered-classes`>>).

''R-CHARTER, the one AID train.'' Its first stage stands landed (train K of #/the-trains): the chairs sign with seat keys of their own and pre-rotate on cold cards, a Nexus answers to `nexus-<sha>`, the floor key founds by a clockless self-signed inception (its next cold key committed since `W29`), per-Nexus keys mint at random, the PersonaGroup stands as a Keyhive group, and the Stable founding act founds a hearth (`lares vessel found --hearth`): the place, persona zero and a PRIVATE seed charter at one seat, with no keyring, every fleet relation bound to that charter's AID, and every harness that founds a hearth founding through it (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook#/found`>>). What stays owed:
* ''The Nexus founding act'': two founders, each from their own vault, seat one key each in a fresh joint genesis at two of two; each reads the other's seat fingerprint aloud; each seat commits its own cold next digest; each founder then signs a carriage contract that both seats countersign (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus`>>). Train N.
* ''The cadence inception'' cited once by a joint Nexus founding, never by a household seed: its shape stands in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/contract`>>, and its holders stand ruled (#/rulings; `K9`).
* ''Boards resolve per relation''; the fleet-join door carries a device edge and one face's grants; add-a-face (A); the bare primary-path seal import goes; the mirror guard keys on posture.

''R-WIRE, the per-Nexus wire key'' (waits on R-CHARTER).
* A per-Nexus wire key in two tiers (operator ruling): a floor route's bundle derives from the floor key and answers at a locked vessel; a sovereign route's bundle derives from the sealed custody root and stands only while the vessel stands unlocked. One gate per (Nexus, route), so knock, pin, verdict and posture read per Nexus exactly (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange`>>). The serving route's third tier rides train E (`E3`).
* ''Noise IK, proof-first'': the gate emits nothing before a valid first message; the knock from `gateKnock` and `knockSegment` in `gate-bundle.ts` selects the sealed face gate, and Noise IK carries message one.
* The floor announcement signs under the wire key, folded in here, so gate and announce never sign under two keys.
* One client dial per (hearth, Nexus), so a vessel walks at more than one hearth; until then a second walk hearth refuses aloud.
* The vessel key stops linking a walker's leaves across Nexuses on the wire (`two-maps` clause 5's residual), and the oracle pointer's vessel-key correlation goes with it (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/pronaos#/the-rung-ladder`).
* ''Per-dial transport keys on the sibling channel'': a leaf proves to a herm under a key fresh per dial, and the leaf-peer transcript binds that key in place of the device key the relay stamps, so a herm colluding with a revoked leaf reads no sibling's device key off its own gate (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/identity-slot-policy#/the-persona-group-secret`).
* ''The admit signature the build owes'': the canon-document list and full sovereign sync `GateBundlePin` enter the signed bytes through the fleet answer and admit carriage; `device-admit.ts` mints the internal `device-admit/v1` edge. The browser's first proof rides `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol` (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof`).
* ''Owed (b), the knock tests offline'': the knock path computes an HMAC-derived segment (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/gate-bundle.ts`, `knockSegment`, `gateKnock`); an observer holding candidate keys tests a sniffed knock offline, so not echoing the key protects only against an observer with no candidates.

''Behind the train, each waiting on R-CHARTER''.
* ''R-KAHU'' stands witnessed: a two-of-two charter whose seats live on two vessels opens, cosigns and lands a cabal admission and a promotion, each vessel under its own scratch root (`nexus-cabal.test.ts`, `nexus-promote.test.ts`). The co-signed `keptBy` fills the realm's steward map (#/rulings).
* ''R-ENGINE, the offers'': the engine-offer lineage signs under the cadence threshold, adopted within a k-hop window along `prev`.
* ''R-EMBER'': a household's second device lifts a resting hearth to SERVING and to nothing more (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember`>>). It needs the fleet-join door; the slot tree carries passphrase and paper pins, and the ember pin binds in a serving tree of its own.

!!! 5 · ONE FACE ~ the recorded switch act (waits on the signer and the realm shapes)
* The slice builds `session-contract.mem` in its build order and `face-proof.mem`. A session wears the face; the vessel wears none. The switch acts on ONE session and closes and re-dials only that session's links under the incoming face's scoped proof.
* ''Working-layer law the build owes'': one working document per (face, wiki, slot kind); its address derives from a secret only that face's vessels hold. The working layer follows its wiki across recipe recomposition. A leaf with an admit and no seat writes nothing to face wikis until join completion. Vessel removal leaves the address standing; the gate and document membership refuse the removed vessel (operator ruling).
* A wiki names one face's composition over bags; sharing happens at the bag (operator ruling).
* A personal Handle never rides without its owning face; one current personal Handle per PersonaGroup; a collective quorum Handle stays separate.
* ''Every hand opens a session'' (operator ruling; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-hands`>>): a tab, a CLI run, the disk watcher and a spirit's MCP seat differ by capability profile and never by key; MCP and the CLI stand as two doors onto one contract. The slice owes the watcher as a leasing session, the seat under a standing grant, the Mask as a record field, and door parity at the session. A tasked spirit comes down to an AI, a Mask and the vessel's seat, attended or standing; the vessel's own key serves as its system identity and the house mints no other.
* The compose hunk in `open-node-vessel.ts` belongs to this slice's owner; cabal runtime work stops at its module boundary.

!!! 6 · TWO HEARTHS ~ the fresh start, then the ceremony (waits on everything above)
* The fresh start by hand (#/fresh-start) on both machines, on the re-found build.
* The ceremony: both found their seeds through the Stable act and stand; both found the Nexus fresh through the Nexus founding act; each signs a contract the two seats countersign; both join one shared cabal realm beside others; each wears a face; an edit crosses both ways and reads back after a restart on each machine. Probes use synthetic names; no fixture forges a cast persona's key or Handle.

!!! The sealed store ~ five steps threaded through the paths (operator-approved model; step one's format stands as law and in code; each later step lands in its place before the fence, under #/the-attractor's ruling)
''Step three's launch kit'': <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/docs/train-s3-launch`>>.
* ''The model'': cleartext lives in the live wiki and in the operator's projected files alone. A private bag rests as authenticated ciphertext chunks, the same bytes at rest and in a blind carrier's hands; a node that holds the capability and stands locked rests sealed. Private bytes take randomized authenticated encryption addressed over the ciphertext, so convergent sealing leaves the private tier. A bag's content key rolls at a causal removal act, never by a clock.
* ''Scope'' (operator ruling): the sealing covers the wiki content stores, node and browser, and the content-addressed store, in carriage as well as at rest. The memory palace, the sensorium, receipts and logs stand outside it. Public and crossroads content stays readable to whoever holds the public keys or lookups.
* ''Faces stand as facets of one operator'' (operator steer): no per-face projection root. Each kind of reader gets named — own vessel, own facet, a friend's face, a hosted guest, a carrier that holds none — before any admission-history rule gets written. One tree on disk serves the person: a projected wiki stands as a surface a session leases, and an edit from disk returns under that session's face (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-hands`>>). Whoever holds the disk reads every facet's projection and may link them.
* ''Step 1, the one private-byte format'': it stands as law at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store`>>, and that carrier names each part the code holds: the sealed value with its ciphertext address and named refusals; the key version, manifest and checkpoint records; the lockbox payload; shareable-history spans; the key lineage and the roll a seat roll feeds. The content-addressed store and the walker's carriage seal and open through it, no opener returns unauthenticated bytes, and no key derives from content or from a Nexus secret. The format changes ONCE, there. The content-key roll stands subscribed to the daemon island's seat rolls. What steps two to five take from the format reads at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/owed`>>.
* ''Step 2, the key's seat'': a content key's lockbox, the store that rests lockboxes, records and the chunk index, and the content-key holder stand as law at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/the-store`>>, and that carrier names each part the code holds. A node vessel's daemon island seats the holder beside its Keyhive identities and subscribes the content-key roll to its own seat rolls; a removal's successor wraps for the readers that remain, each lockbox into a Keyhive document of its own, so a removed device opens nothing a vessel seals after it counts the raise. Owed from this step, each to its owner: the session open that hands the island a signer and the chain check, without which no content key mints on a production vessel; a door from the island to the vessel's fold for the raises it counts; the face join's call for a from-now admission or a hand-over; and the browser shore's rest and Archive writer. A raise over the vessel that founded a face's key group names no seat a roll can leave out, so that vessel keeps its keys until `K12` stands a fresh group. What steps three and four take from this step reads at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/owed`>>.
* ''Step 3, after ONE FACE'': sealed storage values and hydration scoped by the session's face; every fetch, write, catalog lookup and content-addressed read gets checked against that face, since a key alone cannot stop a trusted daemon handing one face another's document.
* ''Step 4, after the KAHU PLANE's seats and wire'': sealed history published to and fetched from a blind carrier by the house's own carriage protocol, with a causal index. A carrier never joins document sync.
* ''Step 5, after ONE FACE'': a public bag served at the floor of a locked node; today the floor serves the oracle snapshot alone.
* ''History at admission'' (operator ruling): sharing a bag's past with a newcomer must have stood declared when those edits got written. An inviter chooses between history the realm already marked shareable and a present-state admission, and never widens the audience for another writer's past. The design target keeps one logical bag name whose key rolls mark epochs: a removal rolls, a from-now admission rolls, and a with-its-past admission hands over the earlier epochs' keys.
* ''Mutual removal'' (operator ruling; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae`>>): both signed acts and both key branches stay, every member sees the conflict, and it ends in an explicit fork unless the realm's charter named a recovery council.

!!! The shore ~ the browser's five rungs, beside the paths
* ''The rungs'': 0, an admitted crossing; 1, a carrier the node holds arrives and renders; 2, a typed field edit reaches the node and survives its restart, read from a fresh browser profile; 3, one human, many faces; 4, two hearths. Rungs 0–2 give the one-machine witness the working-Lararium ruling asks for, and the node's own read decides each rung, never a screenshot or a connected socket.
* ''What blocks rungs 0–2'': rungs 0 and 1 pass live (`tools/browser-ci.sh`, the crossing and render walks); rung 2's leaf half saves and survives a reload of the same profile. ONE FACE's working layer and the durability receipt on the authenticated session stand built (`F3`, `F5`, PR #108), so the node half now waits on a walk that reads it: the hearth's stage walk (`L4`) reads rung 2 whole, page to disk and back, on a node that serves its own page (`L1`) to a page that dials its own origin (`L2`).
* Device capabilities get measured by `tools/capability-probe/` on real devices, never assumed.
* ''What blocks rung 3 onward'': rung 3 waits on ONE FACE. Rung 4 waits on HARBOR's household HTTPS, on the KAHU PLANE's grant for a foreign leaf, and on browser custody: a browser leaf holds a passphrase or paper slot before it holds a persona root (#/rulings).
* The walks ride `tools/browser-ci.sh`, and build under `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom#/the-ruling`: a frame carries absolute state, and a worker defines no global `window`.

!!! The test re-cut ~ train T beside the paths (rows in #/stride-t-recut; kit <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/test-recut-road`>>)
* ''What it builds'': the house's code made testable at the grain its claims live at, before the founding (#/rulings, 2026-10-31): a shared scratch fixture, events where tests poll, pure readers lifted out of the boot composer, instance-owned doors, the composer cut along capability lines into stages both vessel kinds share, and a mutation runner that scores what a test proves.
* ''Four waves'': wave 0 holds the security, custody and correctness cures the cut reads found, each red-first, and a red that stays green retires its row; wave A runs parallel and touches no composer; wave B pins the boot's order and its events; wave C cuts the seams, and `T12`, the composer's stage-cut, runs as the one named single-owner step after `T6`'s characterization pins.
* ''It never stalls a path'': a T row reaches the compose hunks of `open-node-vessel.ts` and `open-browser-vessel.ts` only through a module boundary or through `T12`'s window. The kit holds the measured ground, the god-method's lift map, the floor's talk-story, the reads behind the rows and the laws each row keeps; this carrier holds the rulings and the rows.

!!! Off this road, by ruling
* ''Cloud carriers'': capability and carrier law, private sealed carriage, per-store lifecycle, causal wake and its measurement. Deferred until the operator can deploy; the reading of "delegated keys" (a narrow carriage grant, or a backup that can act) stays open with them.
* ''The Sensorium'': waits behind the working-Lararium milestone. The memory stage (`L8` to `L13`) runs on plain tiddlers with no embedding and no sidecar, and lifts nothing of this wait (#/rulings, 2026-10-31). Its return waits on `G48` (a rewound turn stays recallable) and retires `G47`'s three placeholders.
* ''Streams'' (`saqimtiaz/streams`, BSD-3-Clause): a deferred crossroads offering; it never rides the genesis boot set.

